// Cappfinity Skills Library — 80 behavioural skills (from the Cappfinity Creative skills library)
const LIBRARY_FULL = [{"s": "action", "n": "Action", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Action feel an overriding compulsion to act immediately and decisively. They are much more comfortable with forward momentum than with careful strategy and reflection. As soon as they get an idea, they don’t want to wait. Getting started is the only way for them. If it doesn’t work out, then at least they had a go.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They always think “Why wait?”. If they get a great idea or someone else comes to them with one, they want to act on it straight away.", "They often have a surplus of energy and feel compelled to get going when asked to support on a task.", "If they don’t know the answer to something they are working on, they are quick to enlist the help of others so they can get it done without delay.", "They don’t like to sit on a task and think about it. Getting started is the only way for them. That way, they can see what works and learn quickly from any mistakes."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love acting immediately to support their own goals, initiatives and ideas. They know what to do and who to call on. If they don’t, they know that a way will be found to get started."]], ["In others", ["Support others to take more time to reflect on all possible options. Ask them to explain their chosen course of action so everyone’s clear, that way they can get the buy-in they need."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They can find the planning stage frustrating or are quickly bored when working with others who work slowly."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What energises you most about getting started and acting immediately?", "In what situations does your desire to act quickly reveal itself most strongly?", "How do you deal with situations where you can’t act quickly or where others may procrastinate?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Finish off any projects that have stalled, either personally or in teams. Break down tasks into smaller achievable steps so it always feels like action is being taken.", "Partner with someone who plans, but never seems to get going on a task. Take more of a lead role on new and diverse projects where others might struggle to get going. Take the opportunity to learn new skills.", "Ensure resources are readily available and deadlines and project objectives are clearly understood by everyone, in order to move forward quickly on critical tasks when required."]], ["In your teams", ["Ensure teams are focused on being busy on the right things. Visit the organisation’s strategy and goals regularly, to make sure they are on track with what is required."]]]}, {"s": "adaptable", "n": "Adaptable", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Adaptable love re-arranging resources and adapting their plans to meet changing demands. They sense when to stay on track and when to deviate to become more effective. Their adaptability means that they are always looking for the best possible fit for things, being prepared to change as the context and need requires.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love playing around with things to get the best possible result from re-organising and re-arranging them.", "They enjoy being in the moment of having to re-arrange the way a meeting or event needs to run – perhaps if someone has overrun or cancels.", "They get such a buzz from someone coming to ask them “How on earth will we get that done?” and having the answer.", "They can bounce between groups of team members or form collaborations with different colleagues easily as circumstances dictate."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders adapt their leadership priorities and projects seamlessly to meet senior stakeholder requirements, demonstrating resourcefulness and commitment to challenging situations."]], ["In others", ["Support others to step back and consider their objectives and priorities in order to re-prioritise them most effectively."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not know when to leave well alone, and when a more stable and consistent approach could be more successful."]], ["Coaching questions", ["In what types of projects and situations do you most enjoy being flexible with your approach? Why is this?", "What is it that energises you most about re-arranging and adapting your plans?", "What do you do that allows you to judge how things best fit and what can be adapted?"]], ["Upskilling", ["When a change is required quickly, make decisions collaboratively so that a diverse team can consider both the short- and long-term impact and the outcome required.", "Encourage variety by seeking roles and tasks that require a level head in a fast-paced environment to stretch the skill further. Work on projects with short deadlines or that involve multiple teams.", "When flexibility is required at pace, seek clear direction on changing deadlines and priorities, so attention can be focused in the right areas with more confidence."]], ["In your teams", ["Consider whether change is necessary when the team are asked to adapt. What was wrong with the existing plan? How will the change benefit the future goals of the team?"]]]}, {"s": "adherence", "n": "Adherence", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Adherence have a natural focus on taking the right approach. They love following guidelines, rules, instructions and procedures. They feel most comfortable completing tasks that have clear steps and stages, where they are trusted to follow these to the letter.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They work best when they know what they are supposed to do and when people know what to expect from them.", "They don’t deviate from the rules. If the rulebook says it has to be done in such a way, then it must say it for a good reason.", "People usually feel safe and secure when connecting and working with them. They know what to expect.", "If there isn’t a process for something they are working on, nothing excites them more than coming up with one."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders relish getting things done according to the rules and procedures, even under pressure. They can be strongly relied upon by others to take a methodical approach."]], ["In others", ["Help others to view unstructured situations as opportunities to develop innovative solutions and ways forward."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may prefer tasks and roles where there is some strict structure in place, as they don’t work so well without it."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How does your ability to stick to the rules support you daily?", "What do you do when there are limited guidelines or no guidelines to follow?", "How do you work positively with others who don’t enjoy operating within clear boundaries?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Bring structure and calm to chaos by seeing the positive in rules. Explain the efficiency benefits they will bring and the rationale for the approach to ensure buy-in.", "If there is a rule that people are finding hard to buy into, review how it supports strategic or financial objectives. Seek to understand exactly what is expected of people within the process to allow for consistency.", "Not all rules work. Provide feedback to stakeholders on any processes which seem unnecessary or outdated. Suggest improvements using technology to help people make rules easier to follow."]], ["In your teams", ["Are the team creative or flexible enough when required? Consider ways in which the team have not benefited from rules, or where the rules may need to be tested."]]]}, {"s": "adventure", "n": "Adventure", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Adventure relish the risk of the unusual or the untried. They look forward to experiences that are on the edge of, or outside, their comfort zone. New and challenging scenarios provide ways for them to test themselves and to understand what they can and can’t do. Above all, they love to push the boundaries and see what life will throw at them.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love to try something that others may shy away from due to the risk or uncertain nature of it.", "They find themselves thinking about how they would react to new and difficult situations. It helps them to gain a deeper understanding of themselves.", "They find it exciting to do something that scares them and that carries a real risk – something that takes them to the edge.", "They really enjoy experiencing new and challenging situations, and even more so if it is done with others."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are energised by leadership challenges that push them outside their comfort zone and are invigorated by new experiences and the learning from them."]], ["In others", ["Notice their eagerness to push boundaries. Support them to understand when routine tasks are a priority and when new tasks can take centre stage."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They could become an adventure seeker for the sake of it, which could impact negatively on their wellbeing and see them becoming bored with tasks."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What’s the biggest risk you have taken? How did you benefit from it?", "What gives you that buzz when you’re at the edge of your comfort zone?", "What impact does your sense of adventure have on you or others around you?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Is there a challenge or new task or project that has been put off due to risk or the unknown? What one thing could be done to move it forward? What other skills could support successfully completing the task?", "Create a list of questions when considering a new approach, to weigh up the benefits of choosing either a riskier or a consistent approach. Discuss the results with others to gain consensus and buy-in on team projects.", "Review the goals and aspirations to be achieved in the next year at work. Then add a 20% enhancement to the outcome, for example, shorten the time, increase the profit or try a new approach."]], ["In your teams", ["Not everyone in the team will like to be adventurous. Support the people in the team who are more risk-averse to do more new things gradually. Role model and share the team’s success stories."]]]}, {"s": "analysis", "n": "Analysis", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People strong in Analysis find much satisfaction in systematically analysing everything. They love to interpret problems, numbers and intricate information in detail to come up with the best possible solution or critical decision.", "sec": [["At its best", ["People come to them for their robust analytics, knowing they can be relied upon to have done their research.", "They don’t leave anything to interpretation or a gut feeling. Working out what’s happening is a science for them.", "They enjoy being methodical and meticulous in their approach to assessing data from various sources.", "They don’t just accept things at face value but love to get deep into the data and then question and analyse things thoroughly."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Analysis enjoy taking time to reflect on their leadership style and analysing the judgements and decisions they make and the impact they have on their career, their successes and any learnings."]], ["In others", ["Support others to balance their analytical work with other priority projects. While leaning on them to review information and spot patterns and trends, ensure they have the time available."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not know when they have sufficient data to analyse, which can delay them making a decision. Remind them they may need to make interpretations based on the information they currently have."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What’s your approach to starting the analysis process? What do you start with and what tools support you?", "What types of analysis do you like best? Who does this support the most?", "What piece of analysis are you most proud of and why?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Gain exposure to cross-functional data to broaden an understanding of products or client metrics. Offer to compare both team’s data and make suggestions on further statistical collaboration that would benefit the organisation.", "Work with others to learn how they approach their fact-finding and evidence gathering. What tools or frameworks do they use? Gain knowledge of how they present their statistics in a way that’s engaging to others. Practise and get feedback on articulating complex issues.", "Get involved with problem-solving exercises to practise and extend breaking down complex issues into smaller components where each one can be analysed separately – and different approaches tried. Pay close attention to detail at each stage, asking others to step in if required."]], ["In your teams", ["Help the team avoid focusing too narrowly on being meticulous when analysing data. By leveraging creative ideas and practical perspectives with analytical insights, the team can arrive at well-rounded solutions."]]]}, {"s": "approachable", "n": "Approachable", "f": "Relating", "x": "People strong in Approachable naturally find ways to be open and accessible to others. They foster an environment where others feel they can get support and ask for help. They are keen to welcome people through their open-door policy and find themselves easy to get to know with their friendliness and warmth.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are always readily available for anyone that needs them and plan for interruptions, as many people take them up on their offer of support.", "They love encouraging others to ask them for help and advice and remind them that this can be at any time and through any means.", "They are prompt in responding to others, whether it’s in person, via email or through other communication channels.", "They maintain an open and inviting posture and make eye contact, signalling to others that they are ready to listen and support."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Approachable are known as always being available and willing to make time for those they lead, no matter how busy they are. It is important to them to ensure people feel understood and listened to."]], ["In others", ["Help colleagues balance their ability to drop what they’re working on and chat things through with others and meeting their own deadlines. They may need to work longer hours to get their own work done and this could impact their wellbeing."]], ["Hidden risks", ["Their policy of always being available for people may become a distraction from their work. Suggest they set aside time to be accessible to others, so that they have enough time to concentrate on their own work."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What do you do that makes you seem approachable to others?", "What people do you enjoy being approachable to the most? Why is this?", "How do you protect time when you need to focus on tasks or important projects?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Consider seeking mentorship or coaching from individuals who excel in creating supportive environments. Learning from their experiences and insights can provide valuable guidance for further enhancing open team cultures.", "Be available to colleagues at a specific time each day without an exact task or plan in mind. Let colleagues know that the ‘door will be open’ at that time and then reflect on how being available in this more informal way has made them feel.", "Practise active listening techniques to ensure being fully present and engaged when approached by others. Pay attention to body language and tone of voice to ensure they reflect openness and approachability."]], ["In your teams", ["Being easily approachable is great for collaboration and building relationships but may lead to work overload or feelings of burnout. Coach the team on how to set boundaries to respect their own time and the time of others."]]]}, {"s": "authenticity", "n": "Authenticity", "f": "Being", "x": "Whatever they do, people with a strength in Authenticity do it with genuine feeling and without pretence. They always keep to their own values and beliefs, no matter what people want them to do. Regardless of pressure from others, they are proud to stand up for what they believe in, doing the things that are right for them, in the way that is right for them.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are known for their integrity. People know that they will always follow the path that is right for them and aligned with their values.", "They always behave in a way that’s genuine – they can’t think about behaving in any other way.", "They prefer to be with colleagues that accept them for who they are. It also means they expect others to be themselves in return.", "Sometimes they might be the only one that feels differently about something, but they still have to say something, or they know they will burst."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders excel at being true to themselves in pressurised times. Where others may crack under pressure, leaders lead in a way that upholds their values."]], ["In others", ["Support others to be mindful of the broader context of tasks that they may not agree with. Help them understand that flexibility can be beneficial."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may find it quite difficult to be in a situation that goes against the grain of who they are and what they believe in."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does your strong sense of who you are come from? Has this changed over time?", "Is there a situation when you held firm and maintained your strength in Authenticity that makes you proud?", "How does what you believe in guide your day-to-day actions and decisions?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Regularly share ideas and opinions and embrace situations where honesty may be challenging. Work with a colleague, or a client that requires a direct or more difficult approach, and take time to validate their feelings.", "Accept and understand that it’s OK for beliefs to differ from those of others. Encourage an open and honest style of communication in teams to support challenges and to move on from any mistakes. Focus on the end goal when views differ or are uncomfortable.", "Understand the diverse values and backgrounds of others so there is an awareness of any pushback received and the reason for this. Try to align roles with values, and where this isn’t possible, explain the rationale and partner up people for support."]], ["In your teams", ["Know when they need to stand strong as a team, as well as when they need to flex a little. Remember that other teams may hold different values."]]]}, {"s": "bounceback", "n": "Bounceback", "f": "Motivating", "x": "Whatever the obstacle, setback or disappointment people with a strength in Bounceback come up against, it just makes them more determined to succeed. Following disappointments, they love to pick themselves up very quickly and use setbacks as a springboard to spur them on to prove, to themselves, that they can achieve more than they would have done otherwise.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They have been through some real difficulties in their life, but they have always managed to use them to come out on top.", "When a setback happens, they find themselves saying “I’ll show you” and quickly turning the disappointment into a drive to do even better.", "They feel great looking back at a situation that didn’t go as planned and think “I’ve since done better for myself”.", "They are confident to keep going during any conflict they experience in their relationships. After all, the best ones are worth fighting for."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are able to move forward from personal or business disappointment. Never spending too long dwelling on the situation, they become more determined to succeed."]], ["In others", ["Support others to avoid making the same mistakes after a setback. Give them time to learn from the issue, rather than moving straight on to the next challenge."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may put themselves in too many situations requiring them to recover and do not set aside time to learn from these experiences."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does your ability to pick yourself up from a setback and do even better come from?", "What enables you to maintain your determination to succeed? What resources do you draw on?", "When has your ability to respond positively to setbacks been most helpful to you?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Get involved in a difficult task at work where others have taken a back seat due to the risk or untried nature. Lead with the attitude that failure is learning and learning is knowledge. Ensure the insights are shared.", "Review existing goals and priorities across individuals and teams. Are the outcomes guaranteed or predictable? Could people stretch themselves to achieve a better outcome? Consider new innovations to beat the competition.", "When a setback occurs, consider the best strategies to move forward. Share ideas about what is achievable and by when to manage recovery in a timely but effective way. It’s OK to take time to recover."]], ["In your teams", ["Watch out for those in the team who don’t recover so easily in times of adversity. Give them space and recovery time, as well as encouragement and confidence to move on."]]]}, {"s": "business-thinker", "n": "Business Thinker", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People strong in Business Thinker get how business works. They understand the intricate nature of business operations through their deep curiosity. They are fascinated to uncover ways to improve operations by exploring common themes, values and drivers that predict business success.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They enjoy reading industry publications, attending business conferences and networking to stay updated on the latest trends.", "They love thinking about innovative strategies to enhance processes based on their research and understanding of what drives business success.", "They are energised by discussions that explore the details of business operations, seeking to understand the principles that deliver results.", "You find them reviewing business materials, such as financial reports, sales data, customer feedback or operational metrics, to gain a deeper understanding of business performance."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Business Thinker are fascinated about the intricacies of business processes. They like to further their knowledge and develop their critical thinking skills by asking questions of other business leaders."]], ["In others", ["Help colleagues to review and analyse processes and profit margins, alongside taking action to improve business outcomes. Balance their thirst for knowledge with execution."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may abandon existing successful business processes in favour of a new operation they have researched. Ask them to consider the pros and cons of the new operation and which process will bring greatest success."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What interests you about the world of business?", "What area of business success would you most like to spend your time in?", "Is there an area of business you’d like to develop more in? What could you do to start this?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Get a greater understanding of different business functions to identify opportunities for collaboration and optimisation across departments. Consider developing relationships with professionals across various industries and roles. Networking provides valuable insights, possibilities for partnerships and access to diverse perspectives.", "Sharpen problem-solving skills in the business by approaching challenges with a structured mindset. Break down complex problems into manageable components and explore creative solutions. Utilise existing data-driven insights to identify patterns, trends and areas for improvement within business operations.", "Keep up to date with business developments by continuously staying informed about current trends, advancements, innovations and best practices in various industries. Subscribe to relevant publications, attend seminars and participate in industry forums to expand knowledge."]], ["In your teams", ["Teams that are hyper-focused on business outcomes may lose sight of the people-focused implications. Exercise best practices and work collaboratively when proposing solutions or changes that are sensitive or require people to change."]]]}, {"s": "catalyst", "n": "Catalyst", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Catalyst love to mobilise and inspire other people to take part in activities and projects. They particularly enjoy getting new projects off the ground and putting ideas into action by involving others. They find it so rewarding to get people excited and motivated to work on things that otherwise they might never have done.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They find that new approaches are too exciting to pass them by. They love to get involved in them.", "If a project is going to make a big difference to the way the organisation does things, they are quick to get people on board with their ideas.", "When someone they trust and respect gives them a great idea for a way forward, they find they are immediately getting others excited about it too.", "When they are working on a project with others, they are the ones who will continue to motivate people so that they embrace it as much as they do."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders feel excited and energised when instigating something new. This energy is infectious and a source of inspiration and confidence for those they lead."]], ["In others", ["Help others understand when a current task may need to take priority over something new. Guide them on when is the right time to get involved in new projects."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may need encouragement to invest in relationships as well as motivate others. They may get too excited about the next objective."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What do you love about motivating and inspiring others?", "What allows you to be so successful at getting others going on a project?", "What situation or project have you initiated that you are most proud of?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Create a compelling vision for others to follow when starting off new projects. Set out the purpose and impact and how everyone can contribute meaningfully to the end goal.", "Launch a new initiative with a checklist to ensure a considered approach. How will the change impact others? How will success be measured? Who will benefit? What messages do people need?", "When kick-starting a new initiative, get the right people involved. Include those who have skills in Organiser or Planner to ensure the project is structured and implemented effectively, as well as those with the stakeholder influence that may be required."]], ["In your teams", ["Do the team ever waste time motivating each other to begin unnecessary tasks? Check that their plans are on the right track and keep re-visiting them before they motivate others to join them."]]]}, {"s": "centred", "n": "Centred", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Centred feel as though they look out at the world with confidence. They are grounded in the solid foundation of their sense of self and in the deep knowledge of who and what they are. They love to feel the natural sense of self-assurance that they maintain almost effortlessly in any situation.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love that people ask them to do things all the time that perhaps they wouldn’t do themselves.", "They are known for their calmness in any situation. People have told them that they feel calm just listening to them speak and watching them approach something.", "They are asked to support difficult or new tasks because people know that they will not get into a panic.", "They feel at ease with themselves in most situations. After all, they believe they can only do their best and they often think “What’s the worst that can happen?”."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders thrive in challenging situations as they have full belief in their abilities. They enjoy rising to the challenge and feel energised tackling obstacles, knowing they’ll overcome them."]], ["In others", ["Support others to know that it’s OK to show their motivation, as they may appear less enthusiastic than others at times."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They could convey a lack of enthusiasm or get delayed to move to action at times, which may impact collaborative tasks."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does your inner confidence come from?", "How does your skill in Centred benefit your tasks and projects?", "When has your self-assurance been tested the most? What did you learn?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Pay more attention in difficult or sensitive situations to what is said and how it is said. Take the lead in role modelling a calming tone and language when interacting with others. Show a calm approach for others to follow, even if the environment isn’t so calm.", "Seek situations where the lead can be taken in more complex or high-pressure environments. Work with others to learn different approaches to challenges, perhaps with regard to people and how to navigate the immediate pressure of a situation.", "Focus on solutions during difficult challenges. Try not to dwell on the setback but move forward, embracing ideas from others to take that initial first step. Be creative and use it as an opportunity to try something new."]], ["In your teams", ["Ensure the team’s calmness is not misinterpreted as lack of interest or enthusiasm by other teams or stakeholders. Choose when and where it is required to jump in and share their passion."]]]}, {"s": "change-agent", "n": "Change Agent", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Change Agent are energised by change. They are able to see the real benefits of change and always love to be involved with, and bring about, change in some way or another. They are often an advocate for new developments and their enthusiasm for change enthuses others who may otherwise have been more reluctant.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They get excited by being in situations where they can pretty much do what they want and be able to explore new opportunities.", "They are always keen to ensure they bring others on board with their ideas in a positive way. They want them to feel as excited as they do and buy into the idea.", "They love being involved with a project that nobody else is doing. They want to be the first to change the way they do something.", "It’s not just the process of change that excites them. They love to identify the changes and consider all the benefits it will bring."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are often the first to see the opportunity that change can bring. They sit with their teams to help overcome reluctance or set aside the fears that others may have."]], ["In others", ["Sometimes they may be inclined to make unnecessary changes. Help them to recognise when it’s best to keep on track with the existing approach."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may look to change for the sake of change, rather than learning when change is necessary. This could lead to instability and uncertainty for other people."]], ["Coaching questions", ["When were you first aware of being energised by change?", "How does change benefit you and those around you?", "How do you get others positively on board when working with a change project?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Ensure changes are well considered and researched with regard to how they will impact people in the short and long term. Understand what support others will need to embrace the change positively.", "Build collaborations across different teams and stakeholders to ensure change is meaningful. Get like-minded and diverse people together to address shared goals and challenges.", "Change doesn’t always need to be big. Consider small, easier to adopt changes and projects in the areas that address current challenges or cost savings, or deliver improved engagement."]], ["In your teams", ["Be selective about the team’s priorities for change. Choose to invest their resources and enthusiasm in the initiatives that are most likely to succeed and make a difference."]]]}, {"s": "collaboration", "n": "Collaboration", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People strong in Collaboration much prefer working with others, benefitting from the collective expertise and skills of colleagues. They flourish in achieving results as a team, both personally through the richness of connection and through the results this achieves.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They approach challenges as a team, leveraging the collective expertise and creativity of the group to find innovative solutions.", "They actively engage in group discussions, meetings and activities and encourage others to do the same, so everyone feels part of the team.", "They proactively reach out to others for their skills to ensure their projects and tasks are successful.", "They love to look for opportunities to collaborate and support others. They know this brings the best results for everyone and is more enjoyable."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Collaboration love to seek the input from their colleagues when it comes to delighting their stakeholders. They value the feedback of others and welcome it to build their development plan."]], ["In others", ["Whilst colleagues may work well to ensure diverse people work together effectively, they may need help to see when individuals need to be encouraged to come up with their own solutions too."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may have a tendency to spend a lot of time looking for advice from colleagues. Encourage them to trust their own expertise and not always rely on help from others."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What do you find are the benefits to you and your work of collaborating with others?", "Who could you find to collaborate with outside of your team to learn something new or expand your knowledge?", "Who do you find yourself collaborating with most? How could you stretch the circle of people you collaborate with?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Reflect on any past experiences where it was difficult to work with someone. Why was it difficult? What could be done to overcome this if working with someone similar in future? Consider how skills and strengths were aligned, or perhaps mis-aligned, and how more productivity could have been achieved together.", "Identify struggling individuals or teams that may need additional guidance. Actively encourage their contributions, listening to their perspectives and challenges to understand how to support them in the best possible way.", "Seek out opportunities to work on diverse or group projects across different parts of the business. These experiences will provide greater personal and business learning as well as help navigate group dynamics in the future."]], ["In your teams", ["Broadening individual expertise areas will also strengthen the overall team and what they can collaborate on. Promote personal responsibility by helping align individuals with tasks they are good at and enjoy doing."]]]}, {"s": "commercial-insight", "n": "Commercial Insight", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People strong in Commercial Insight are innately attuned to the bottom line, instinctively focusing on tangible outcomes that drive commercial success. They love to find opportunities to influence the profitability of projects with different perspectives and practical solutions.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They naturally make an effort to observe the financial benefits of any initiative.", "They have a deep understanding of commercial principles, including market dynamics, financial metrics and industry trends.", "They spend time getting to know their competitors and market well so that they can identify ways to influence clients commercially.", "They actively seek opportunities to optimise project outcomes and influence their profitability."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Commercial Insight will drive focus in their teams to the commercial ‘bottom line’. They will help their colleagues to identify ways that enhance and optimise profitability."]], ["In others", ["Support colleagues to raise relevant concerns about profitability and commercial accountability while considering the broader picture of the organisation’s values and culture."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may put too much pressure on themselves to win new commercial opportunities, which can impact their health. Encourage them to look after their own wellbeing when pursuing commercial success."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Which recent project provided an opportunity for you to demonstrate an improvement in the bottom line?", "What is your approach to reviewing profit and loss?", "What excites you about improving a profit margin for a project or organisation?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Seek out opportunities to acquire knowledge of business economics in general and get a more in-depth understanding of key commercial concepts and frameworks. This will help develop a greater understanding of what affects overall financial performance.", "Identify and read resources on ‘how businesses are commercially successful’ and consider broadening sources to include material that provides a commentary on market conditions and the status of competitors.", "Get more familiar with financial statements and key financial metrics and consider how to improve commercial impact. View these along with the organisational goals and mission to ensure there is alignment."]], ["In your teams", ["Think realistically and critically about how proposed solutions will be operationalised and their alignment with the business mission – a commercial win is only a win if it is sustainable in the long run."]]]}, {"s": "compassion", "n": "Compassion", "f": "Relating", "x": "People with a strength in Compassion have an open heart and care about all the people around them. They want the best for everyone, and offer sympathy and support to others, especially in times of suffering. When people are unhappy, they look for the right thing to say and aim to help people in whatever way they can.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They care deeply about the people they work with. They want to make sure they are happy and doing well.", "People tend to come to them because they know that they care. It doesn’t matter what the problem is – they will just want to help.", "They feel it deeply when someone is suffering. When they can support them, they get a huge sense of satisfaction.", "Nothing gives them a greater feeling than knowing they have helped someone on the road to recovery. They like to make a difference."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders know the importance of role modelling genuine compassion to everyone, giving people permission to stop and show they care, no matter how busy they are or what position they have."]], ["In others", ["Help them to recognise when to be proactive and offer their compassion and when to allow others to come to them for help."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They must be careful not to be too overwhelming by reaching out without an invite. They may need to learn when compassion may not be helpful and balance it with practical support and advice."]], ["Coaching questions", ["In what situations do you find yourself using your Compassion skill the most?", "What impact does your strength in Compassion have on you and on those around you?", "How do you deal with others who are less compassionate than you?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Take an active role when resolving sensitive challenges. Look beyond the scope of the task and into how decisions will impact people’s lives more holistically. Champion and role model appreciation and consideration of others at work.", "When people are experiencing difficulty, help to find the balance between caring and moving people on to an action they can embrace that will support their recovery and healing. This will build future resilience too.", "Sometimes less is more to show you care. Ask thoughtful questions, listen, smile and follow up with people. Take confidence that caring doesn’t mean needing to have all the answers, and that being approachable and empowering go a long way."]], ["In your teams", ["While still caring deeply, use the team’s foresight to be proactive in providing support in more focused ways. Follow up with people and help them with an action plan to move forward."]]]}, {"s": "competitive", "n": "Competitive", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Competitive just have to compete against others. Measuring their abilities relative to others is how they gauge progress and success. They love to make everything into a contest. They want to perform quicker and better than everyone at everything they do. For them, winning is the only option. Losing hurts.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are a very competitive person. They want to be the best at what they do.", "Whatever they are doing, they find themselves starting to think about winning and how to beat the competition.", "Even if it is only small, they like to have something they can regularly call a win to make them feel as though they are competing.", "People are often surprised at how they can bring competitive skills into everything they do."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are really motivated when their performance can be successfully compared against others. They’ll do everything they can to avoid being second best."]], ["In others", ["Encourage others to review what they’re doing to ensure it’s effective as well as focused on the end goal of winning."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not realise when competition is unhealthy or impacts a more collaborative approach to team tasks."]], ["Coaching questions", ["When did you first notice your competitive edge?", "What does being competitive achieve for you within your work projects and tasks?", "When, or in what situations, are you at your competitive best?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Look for different ways to actively measure what is achieved, either personally or as a team. Take time to understand how the win was accomplished and to celebrate the successes.", "Introduce fun ways at work to be more competitive. Discover any hidden competitiveness with colleagues or teams that encourages targets. Recognise all wins, not just the obvious ones – for example, acknowledging when someone tries something new.", "Consider competition alongside the personal, team and organisational goals to ensure they have value. Seek to stretch an existing task or project by 10% or reduce a deadline."]], ["In your teams", ["Look out for those in the team who are less competitive. Make sure the competitive ‘games’ that people play do not ride roughly over their feelings or ability to get involved."]]]}, {"s": "connector", "n": "Connector", "f": "Relating", "x": "Whatever situation they find themselves in, people with a strength in Connector always love to make connections between the people that they meet. They notice when people have shared interests or something in common. They naturally make links between people and bring them together for their mutual benefit.", "sec": [["At its best", ["At networking events, they are known for constantly introducing people to each other. They have a quick chat and move on quickly to see who else they can connect.", "They love bringing the right people together so that everyone has a good time and gets something from the meeting.", "They spend a lot of time thinking about who comes to the work events or meetings that they arrange. They like to ensure that the people there have something in common with each other.", "It’s even more rewarding when they bring a diverse group together who don’t appear to have much in common. They get such a buzz when they connect."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders enjoy sharing interests, knowing that people and their connections can help them progress their career in both the short and longer term."]], ["In others", ["Helps others to build relationships as well as make those connections with people. Encourage them to get to know people for deeper connections."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may forget to connect with individuals themselves on a deeper level and spend quality time getting to know them."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What motivates you to make connections between people?", "What strategies do you use to build connections between people?", "When has bringing people together made the biggest difference for you?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Consider the longer-term goals and any useful connections that could be made today that would reap benefits in the future. Lean into other people’s networks too.", "When using technology and social media, ensure interactions with others are meaningful. Connect with purpose and be clear on what the end goal is for both parties, so the right support is offered.", "As connections are made, grab a coffee together or be sure to keep in touch regularly on a variety of subjects. It takes time to build relationships and lots of active listening and curiosity."]], ["In your teams", ["Think about how the team networks with stakeholders throughout your organisation. How can they work together to be more strategic in the networking that they do as a team?"]]]}, {"s": "counterpoint", "n": "Counterpoint", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Counterpoint love to bring an alternative perspective to any situation. They seem to see things differently from others and can present a range of options, possibilities and alternatives for any scenario. As a result, they often bring things into the discussion that other people have missed.", "sec": [["At its best", ["Whenever they are in a meeting, they find they need to sit on their hands until the time is right to share their idea. Not saying something is always a regret.", "They love to put forward different approaches to a discussion or situation. It can often be anything, they love to get involved in most subject matters.", "The excitement for them is to bounce different ideas around and get people to think about things in different ways.", "They don’t need to be an expert in a topic to have an idea about it. They use their instincts and experiences to guide what feels right."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love being the person who looks at things through a different lens. Leaders know that taking a new perspective can help them generate fresh ideas or solve difficult problems."]], ["In others", ["Encourage them to share views at the right time to avoid slowing projects down. Consider ways they can focus on when and how they add value."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may find it difficult to know when not to be different, and when they should be prepared to join the majority consensus. This could come across as annoying or obstructive."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What drives you to see things differently to other people?", "What benefits does your skill in Counterpoint bring to a discussion or situation?", "How do others react to you sharing views and ideas on many topics?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Don’t just come up with the ideas but also formulate a plan and a business case. That will be far more credible, and the idea is more likely to go ahead as people can get on board quicker when knowing what to expect.", "Seek out opportunities to be a guide, mentor or adviser to others. Although the answer may not always be known, practise offering advice, ideas and approaches and receiving feedback on these.", "Find regular opportunities to share thoughts and opinions to a diverse group of people. Create an open environment to combine ideas or support partially developed views."]], ["In your teams", ["Consider the benefits of an alternative perspective. Is it relevant to the conversation? Does it add value? Think about timing – is it too late? Will it hinder the team?"]]]}, {"s": "courage", "n": "Courage", "f": "Being", "x": "While they may well feel afraid at times, people with a strength in Courage are always able to face and overcome their fears. They get a buzz from participating in activities that make them nervous or scared. They never let their fear get in the way of what they want to do.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They like new experiences and are comfortable with the unknown. They may not always enjoy it but will face the challenge anyway.", "Overcoming their fears comes from within them. Their heart volunteers for something before their head kicks in with the reality.", "They get as scared as others in challenging situations, but they push themselves not to allow the fear to hold them back. The results are very satisfying, despite the sweat and tears.", "They don’t take unnecessary risks, but they might find they say or do something that could be a bit unpopular, as they aren’t worried what people think."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are happy making difficult decisions in the best interests of their goals and priorities. Taking the easy way out is not an option for them and they have faith in their decisions."]], ["In others", ["Help them to know when the time is right to engage in fear-raising experiences. Encourage time-outs to create moments of calm."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may prefer the addiction of fear-raising experiences and avoid moments of calm, which could negatively impact their wellbeing."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How does your Courage help you to achieve your daily goals?", "When have you felt the most scared but have gone ahead anyway? What did you achieve?", "What enables you to face your fears when you are nervous?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Face fears in areas that serve goals rather than for the sake of the thrill. Make informed choices that serve a purpose and have clear benefits to the organisation’s products or services.", "What still sits on the to-do list because of some element of fear? Set a realistic goal linked to it and enlist the support of others to get going. Go in with the attitude that any learning is knowledge for the future.", "Explore what might limit an ability to take on riskier projects or tasks. What support or resources can be offered? Build confidence gradually by finding the right stretching tasks that align with other skills."]], ["In your teams", ["Ensure the team are not taking unnecessary risks and are doing their planning in advance. Take time so that the team can consider all the facts in every situation and understand the risks fully."]]]}, {"s": "creativity", "n": "Creativity", "f": "Thinking", "x": "Creativity is at their core. People with a strength in Creativity love coming up with or combining new ideas, images, colours, tastes or concepts. They thrive on trying things that have not been tried before, linking things in novel and imaginative ways, and creating something.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They get quite excited when others get stuck for ideas on a project. They can’t help getting involved by encouraging others to think differently.", "They are always thinking of ways that they can arrange things in a different way, and then they can’t wait to get started on it.", "They enjoy making dull chores more interesting by being more imaginative about their approach to the task.", "Whether they are preparing for a meeting or a presentation, they love to combine different things to come up with something new."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love coming up with diverse ways to solve problems and are confident to take risks and try out new approaches to do so. They know there’s usually another way."]], ["In others", ["Help them to understand the best times and tasks for using their creativity. Ensure they balance their creative thinking with their other responsibilities."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may create additional work for themselves that was unnecessary. Sometimes they may need to plan and deliver existing approaches."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How often are you able to use your strength in Creativity? Would you like to use this skill more?", "What’s your incentive for doing things in a new and different way?", "When has your Creativity skill made the biggest difference to you or others?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Try to ensure work is varied with opportunities to do different tasks. Work with new people on different client projects or across other teams. It will create more opportunities to apply ideas.", "While coming up with new approaches, be sure to research the best way forward. Review likely costs and the impact it will have so it is received positively and buy-in is more likely.", "Create the environment to encourage creativity. Have periodic agenda-less meetings to explore ideas on projects and ways of working. Vary the topics and discussion style to suit all audiences."]], ["In your teams", ["Ensure they spend enough time generating ideas before the start of a project. Have the future at the front of the team’s mind. Consider a creative venue and an energetic time of day."]]]}, {"s": "credibility", "n": "Credibility", "f": "Being", "x": "People strong in Credibility inspire the confidence and trust of others through the depth of their knowledge and expertise. Their consistent professional conduct is viewed as model behaviour by others, who find themselves seeking their wisdom and guidance regularly. They are skilled in presenting information that captures the attention of their audience.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They seem to just naturally come across as professional when presenting information, as they are clear and concise and have a compelling manner.", "They make themselves available to provide guidance, support and mentorship to those seeking their expertise.", "They are often seen researching and reading up on topics. They know it is the only way to ensure they stay ahead in their area of expertise.", "They set a positive example that there is always more to learn. They embrace all information and are quick to ask questions to further their knowledge."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders with a strength in Credibility will gain the confidence of their team because of their knowledge and expertise. Their team will trust them to guide and develop them in their specific technical or business area."]], ["In others", ["Support others to balance their thirst for additional knowledge in their area of expertise with that of expanding their learning into new areas which could benefit changes in markets and organisational direction."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may put too much faith in their knowledge and expertise from time to time. Recommend that they seek advice from knowledgeable colleagues, so that they can get another perspective."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What do you think it is about you that builds trust and makes you credible with others?", "What gives you that confidence to share your expertise with others?", "Are there people at work you would like to build further credibility with? How might you go about this?"]], ["Upskilling", ["When making suggestions or facing challenges, take time to find best practice evidence-based solutions and share these with colleagues, providing supporting evidence to help increase their confidence in these.", "Communicate clearly and concisely and try to establish and maintain meaningful relationships with a wider range of people. A strong online presence will enhance credibility but ensure that any profiles positively reflect values and expertise.", "Establish a strong personal brand that’s consistent. Incorporate strengths and skills alongside a sense of competence and a well-informed, reliable and consistent approach to building knowledge."]], ["In your teams", ["Rather than rely on the most credible individuals to represent the voice of the team, encourage collaboration and development among all team members. Ensure that everyone feels empowered to become experts in their areas."]]]}, {"s": "curiosity", "n": "Curiosity", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Curiosity are interested in everything. They are totally open to new ideas and constantly seek out new information. They get excited when they discover new topics to study. They never let fascinating points pass them by without finding out more about them. They love to ask questions and do follow-up reading on things.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love to ask questions to find out more. They find themselves thinking of the next question before they’ve got the answer to the first.", "They are always keen to learn more about situations. They don’t take things at face value and always want to know more – exactly when and how.", "They enjoy talking to people about their experiences, however big or small. They love to get the intricate details, especially if they’re about something they don’t know much about.", "They are always reading various news stories and articles. They feel they never know what they might learn or how they might be able to apply the findings."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are interested in learning more about how they can be a better leader. They actively request feedback about their leadership style and how they can improve it."]], ["In others", ["Help them to see when curiosity may impact their other tasks. Suggest times for learning or saving it for projects with longer deadlines."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not always act on their ideas or learnings, or their ability to get things done could be negatively affected."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What benefits does your skill in seeking out new information bring?", "What do you love about being curious and interested?", "What was the last subject you researched? How did you apply this learning?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Focus on research topics wisely to get the most from the learning. What are the current goals or challenges at work? What would be good to know about the organisation’s competitors?", "Find activities where questioning is key. Try interviewing, coaching mentoring, researching, or supporting a colleague to get to the root of a problem.", "Be active in imparting knowledge regularly. Consider a variety of forums for different audiences so valuable learnings are shared with others and feedback on their impact or use is given."]], ["In your teams", ["Be aware of the goals and priorities they have as a team. They may need to focus on what is required that day, without further investigating or questioning the topic."]]]}, {"s": "customer-champion", "n": "Customer Champion", "f": "", "x": "People strong in Customer Champion advocate on behalf of others to ensure their perspectives and interests are heard and acted upon. They seize opportunities to actively speak up and represent customers and stakeholders to those who can take their ideas forward.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love to speak up and advocate for customers and stakeholders to ensure they are considered in decision-making.", "They maintain a customer-centric mindset, constantly seeking ways to improve the customer experience.", "They proactively identify and address issues and challenges faced by customers and stakeholders, sharing them with the relevant teams.", "They actively solicit feedback through surveys, interviews and focus groups, using this information to improve the customers’ journey of a product or with the organisation."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders with a strength in Customer Champion will ensure that their teams have a strong focus on outcomes for customers. They will consistently remind their teams how changes will impact end users of business products and services."]], ["In others", ["Support others to balance the voice of the customer with the workload of their colleagues. Constant improvements could result in teams feeling overwhelmed and focused on one area only, ignoring other priorities."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may focus too much on the needs of the customer, which can be to the detriment of the organisation. Remind them to keep a balance between what the customer wants and the mission of the organisation."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do you currently represent and serve customers in your role?", "When did you last go above and beyond to advocate for a customer? What happened?", "How do you or your teams benefit from ensuring you represent your customers’ needs?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Focus on identifying practical solutions and actionable recommendations to address customer issues and improve their experience. Escalate issues higher if needed and think creatively to find win-win solutions for all parties involved.", "Deepen an understanding of customer needs, preferences and budgets. Gather feedback and insights directly from customers and stakeholders about their pain points and collaborate cross-functionality to see how offerings can be improved.", "Cultivate strong and empathic relationships with customers, stakeholders and internal teams. Empower them by providing the information, confidence and resources they need to advocate for themselves."]], ["In your teams", ["Excessive advocacy for customer needs may strain team dynamics if not balanced with considerations of feasibility and team resources. Consider wellbeing for the team and build in customer improvements over time."]]]}, {"s": "detail", "n": "Detail", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Detail naturally focus on the smallest detail and easily spot inconsistencies and mistakes. They get a great sense of satisfaction when they are able to check that details are accurate and complete. Paying attention to detail is very important to them – they would never submit anything themselves that contained a mistake.", "sec": [["At its best", ["It doesn’t matter what they are doing, what they are working on, or where they are, errors just seem to jump out at them.", "People often ask them to check their work, as they know they get a real buzz from being able to correct errors.", "They always make sure that everything they do is properly finished off and that there are no mistakes.", "Every email they send, they check through to make sure that the spelling and grammar are correct."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders lead their teams with a compelling reputation for being accurate and getting every small detail correct in their plans, reports and communications."]], ["In others", ["Help others to see when they could become too focused on the detail of certain tasks and lose sight of the bigger picture."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not know when to settle for ‘good enough’ or spot when a situation demands them to focus less on detail and more on execution."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Have you always loved to spot errors? Why do you think this is?", "In what situation has your skill in Detail been the most valuable to you?", "How do you balance your focus on detail with your ability to deliver on time?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Be a role model rather than a critic. Create clear, structured guidelines for people at work, to support others in creating error-free work. Use tools and technology and take the time needed.", "Partner Detail people with those strong in Action, Catalyst or Creativity. The powerful combination of great ideas being carefully implemented and considered will shine through.", "Focus a Detail skill on areas of expertise or enjoyment. Try to eliminate distractions and build time to check work or have others review it."]], ["In your teams", ["Don’t get stuck in reviewing or proofreading everything the team create when it isn’t necessary. Work can sometimes be in a phase of creation that doesn’t need complete accuracy at that moment."]]]}, {"s": "diligence", "n": "Diligence", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People strong in Diligence stay attentive to intricate tasks. They naturally focus and enjoy producing quality and precise outcomes. They execute tasks meticulously and have a keen eye for accuracy, even when faced with repetitive work. They care about their results.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They have the natural ability to concentrate deeply on tasks at hand, minimising distractions and staying fully engaged in their work.", "They have a keen eye for accuracy and precision, taking care to avoid errors and inconsistencies.", "They maintain a systematic approach to their work, organising tasks and priorities in a logical and efficient manner.", "Colleagues and managers trust them to consistently deliver high-quality work with minimal supervision."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Diligence will be keen to see a high level of quality and accuracy in their own work and that of their team. They role model tools and frameworks to keep them on track."]], ["In others", ["Help colleagues balance achieving careful precision and high quality in their work with the demands of other key objectives or urgent deliverables."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may become so used to carrying out repetitive tasks that they don’t look to innovate. Encourage them to look at ways to streamline repetitive processes, so that they can focus on more complex tasks."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What types of tasks do you get involved in that require detail and quality?", "What is your approach to remaining focused on repetitive work?", "How do others rely on you for your dedication to accuracy?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Think about situations where it was difficult to keep focused. What was learned from those situations? Think about how to maintain clarity and the steps required to ensure that interest is maintained, regardless of how engaging it is. Have the end goal in mind.", "Develop habits that support the ability to maintain concentration by minimising interruptions that might affect the quality of work. Establish clear boundaries with colleagues. Put ‘do not disturb’ on devices and divide your day between focused tasks, meetings and breaks.", "Use techniques to maintain focus, such as setting clear goals, prioritising tasks, allocating specific blocks of time for focused work on particular tasks, concentrating on a single task until it’s done or reaches a designated stop time, and breaking tasks down into more manageable steps."]], ["In your teams", ["Excessive diligence may slow down progress or hinder team efficiency. Remember to take a step back from the details to see the bigger picture and consider innovative solutions."]]]}, {"s": "drive", "n": "Drive", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Drive are extremely self-motivated. They have an inner drive and motivation that pushes them to achieve more. They never need to be told what to do next. As soon as they complete one task they move on to the next. They love to set their own goals and targets, which are often higher than those that others may have set for them.", "sec": [["At its best", ["As soon as they have finished one task and ticked it off, they are already thinking “What’s next?”.", "They love to be busy. If they don’t have much going on, it doesn’t take long for them to find a task and get going again.", "They have a to-do list that they love looking at and rewriting. The best part is crossing things off it at the end of the day – no matter how small.", "They only feel like they have had a good day if they have achieved something. They don’t like half-finished tasks."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love to set themselves challenges, working hard to be successful. It appears to others that they have an endless appetite for getting things done."]], ["In others", ["Support them to push forward when necessary to avoid exhaustion. Encourage time out to relax and to reflect on their recent successes before the next task."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not know when to just enjoy the moment, or how to strive for a balance that meets not just their desire to achieve things but also their wider life needs and interests."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does your inner motivation and drive to succeed come from?", "What enables you to continually maintain your strength in Drive?", "When has your determination to succeed been most helpful to you?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Reflect on how other skills play their part in success. Don’t just rely on determination Allow the drive to come from inspiration, in order to be focused and take the right action that will deliver the results required.", "Slow down and consider what is to be achieved in the longer term. What can be worked on today that will make way for this? Create a compelling vision so that motivation is always working towards a purpose.", "Work with others to support ambitions. Be around a variety of people who can support, encourage, stretch and give feedback along the way to overcome any challenges."]], ["In your teams", ["As a team, take the time out to determine exactly what you want to achieve, before becoming driven to achieve it. Don’t waste energy pushing yourselves when there isn’t yet a clear goal in sight."]]]}, {"s": "emotional-awareness", "n": "Emotional Awareness", "f": "Relating", "x": "People with a strength in Emotional Awareness are an excellent judge of people’s emotions and feelings. They want to know how people are feeling. They have a keen eye and an intuitive ear. They can easily pick up on subtle cues and messages that people give out, making their emotions very clear to them.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They often notice if somebody is not happy, feeling slightly stressed, or just not themselves.", "They just seem to have this emotional barometer with people. Others often talk to them about difficult and personal situations.", "It’s easy for them to connect with others and they are sensitive to minor emotional cues. They like to help others look for these.", "They find they can judge not only what to say to people but also how to say it, depending on other people’s moods."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are an expert at making sound and informed decisions, understanding how each team member or colleague is feeling about a situation or task."]], ["In others", ["Help others to be mindful of enjoying the moment and the natural interaction and spontaneity with team members."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may stifle their natural interaction with others or rob situations of their spontaneous nature, as opposed to taking people just as they are."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do others respond to your Emotional Awareness strength?", "What helps you become aware of people’s emotions and feelings?", "In what situations does being aware of the feelings of others benefit you most?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Support situations involving conflict. Pick up on any unsaid comments and understand any emotional reaction. Establish whether a group or one-to-one environment is the best place to help any sensitive topics.", "Take a flexible approach when working with people’s emotions. Some may need empowering or motivating to take action, while others may prefer a sympathetic ear only.", "Understand the methods used to pick up on other people’s feelings and build on this. People may change their tone, language, behaviour, facial expressions or attention span when they are unhappy."]], ["In your teams", ["Sometimes the team will need to push forward and focus on urgent tasks. Consider whether the time and environment are right, when it comes to talking about how individuals and the team are feeling."]]]}, {"s": "empathic", "n": "Empathic", "f": "Relating", "x": "People with a strength in Empathic have a natural connection with other people in a way that makes them finely tuned in to them and able to feel what they are feeling. They love to put themselves in another person’s shoes, to experience the emotions they are experiencing. They share their joy, their pain, their frustration and their elation.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They seem to feel just what other people are feeling. Happy or sad, they are there with them, laughing or crying.", "They are always able to see things from another person’s point of view. Doing this helps them to understand that person or the situation better.", "They are able to connect with others easily by expressing their genuine concern for them.", "They imagine what they would do in a situation, whether it’s on television or in real life. They often learn a lot about themselves that way."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders have a natural way of putting themselves in someone else’s shoes when others are experiencing highs or lows, helping them build trusting and lasting relationships."]], ["In others", ["Support them to know when they may be taking on too many problems that belong to other people. This could affect their wellbeing or productivity."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may fail to protect their own emotional state and become overwhelmed by other people’s distress, rather than distancing themselves."]], ["Coaching questions", ["When did you first notice your ability to see things from other people’s points of view?", "How do you build that special connection with people?", "When is being empathic most useful to you at work?"]], ["Upskilling", ["When offering support, ensure a situation is always left knowing what action the person is going to take. Follow this up to help them reach an outcome, rather than just listen.", "Encourage others to share their feelings and motivations towards tasks or situations when appropriate. This will give everyone an opportunity to share how they are really feeling and allow any necessary support to be understood.", "Consider different ways to be empathic based on the person. It may not take grand gestures. Ask open-ended questions, be present, and use empathic language that validates their feelings and shows understanding."]], ["In your teams", ["Anticipate how the team can support each other in more difficult or longer projects. The team may show their nervousness in different ways, so take time to understand exactly what the fear is."]]]}, {"s": "enabler", "n": "Enabler", "f": "Relating", "x": "People with a strength in Enabler enjoy developing people to do things for themselves. At the same time as providing support and encouragement, they love to give people tasks and challenges that they know will stretch them, pushing them outside their comfort zone in ways that will help them to grow and develop.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are known for always saying “You can do it” to people. They genuinely believe that, with the right support, they can.", "It gives them great satisfaction to see people become better and more confident at what they do.", "They really enjoy bringing out the best in people. They gradually give others more stretching jobs and then watch them grow and develop.", "If they aren’t able to develop others, they’ll still spot bursts of talent and tell the right people. They feel it would be a shame to waste it."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders understand the importance of creating the conditions for others to grow and develop, allowing them freedom to be strategic and focus on other priorities."]], ["In others", ["Support them to understand the balance of enabling others with recognising situations that require a more direct approach to get the job done."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may need to be mindful not to push too far or too fast. They may need to know when to let people find their own level and pace."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do others react to the stretching challenges you set them?", "What do you wish to achieve by giving people new challenges?", "What is your proudest success story of when you have enabled someone?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Take the opportunity to develop new people in the team or organisation. Help others to hit the ground running by teaching them how things are done, who’s who, and how to make an impact.", "Be specific in any feedback to support the growth of others. What was observed? How did it benefit others? What skills were they using? What was the impact?", "Consider creating partnerships in teams as a way to mentor or coach others on any lack of skills. Who could learn from who in a practical and engaging way?"]], ["In your teams", ["Ensure that others want to be helped or guided by people in the team. Spot when someone is happy in their comfort zone and consider whether it is the right time for them to push further."]]]}, {"s": "equality", "n": "Equality", "f": "Relating", "x": "Being fair and equitable is at the heart of who they are. People with a strength in Equality consider everyone to be their equal and give great attention to issues of fairness and equality. They love to make sure that all people are treated equally and that their own actions and decisions are as fair as they possibly can be.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They absolutely believe that everyone should be treated fairly and equally, no matter what.", "Whatever they are doing, they strive to get a fair outcome for all. They love to create win-win situations where everyone is treated equally and with respect.", "They are uncomfortable in situations where winning creates a loser, as it can result in negativity and hurt feelings.", "They hold in high esteem those leaders who demonstrate and role model equality and fairness in all that they do."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders rarely think about equality as, for them, there is no other way. Their teams all hold them in high esteem for the natural way they’re fair to everyone."]], ["In others", ["Support them to manage any situations where it is more difficult to treat everyone equally. Help them to identify which situations it is best to challenge, and which may need to be accepted."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may be equal for the sake of it, rather than considering what is best for individuals, the situation or the results to be achieved."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What is it that drives you to make sure that people are treated equally?", "What impact does your focus on fairness and equality have on others?", "How do you respond to others who are less focused on fairness than you are?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Consider ways to get involved in resourcing task allocation or project management. Ensure any friction is alleviated and allow for fair involvement for everyone.", "Celebrate regularly as a team and recognise all contributions. Appreciate different perspectives, ideas and skills and how people’s backgrounds play a part in their success.", "Try to feel comfortable and confident to speak up if a decision is believed to be unfair. Point out anything overlooked from a non-judgemental view. This may allow issues to be seen that others have simply forgotten."]], ["In your teams", ["It is not always possible for work responsibilities to be distributed fairly. The team may need to prioritise the tasks at hand above their perception of fairness."]]]}, {"s": "esteem-builder", "n": "Esteem Builder", "f": "Relating", "x": "Their words and actions help to build self-confidence and self- esteem in others. People with a strength in Esteem Builder see potential and possibility in people and help them to recognise it for themselves. Through their relationships, they give people an understanding of what they’re good at, even when they don’t recognise it themselves.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love to help people recognise what they’re capable of, particularly people who might find it hard to accept positive feedback.", "They naturally look for ways to build people’s confidence and self- esteem. It’s so rewarding when others get to achieve something they never thought they would.", "They are always coming across people who don’t believe in themselves. They look for subtle ways to share what they’ve seen them do.", "They naturally correct people’s language so it turns into more of a ‘can do’ and ‘they can’ attitude – even people they don’t know."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders enjoy helping others they lead to recognise their own potential by sharing and reinforcing positive feedback about their achievements."]], ["In others", ["Help them to be aware of the amount of time they spend supporting others. Suggest they create their own guidelines for when it may be best to take a step back or enable others."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may over-flatter and be interpreted as being inauthentic. They may compromise their authenticity as they help others to develop their self-belief."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What is it that energises you about helping people see what they are capable of?", "How, specifically, do you build people’s understanding of what they can do?", "What is your proudest moment of using your Esteem Builder skill?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Make sure any confidence boosting approach is adapted to the audience. It may need to be more subtle in some contexts to come across as sincere, or be repeated and followed-up on for those who are struggling.", "Try Strengthspotting when giving feedback. What led the person to be successful in that situation? Be specific about what was observed and the impact it had so that it sticks and feels genuine.", "Create a culture where regularly sharing informal feedback is encouraged. Recognise potential as well as small wins – for example, an idea or something new that was tried."]], ["In your teams", ["Praising people too often can be perceived as being inauthentic. The team could work in pairs and have a ‘boost buddy’. Get to know others well, so you know just when you need to follow up with praise."]]]}, {"s": "explainer", "n": "Explainer", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Explainer love to simplify things so that people can easily understand. They can take a complex idea and express it simply and clearly, so it is accessible to a wide range of people. If someone doesn’t understand their explanation the first time, they enjoy coming up with different ways of explaining it.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love being able to take some of the more technical areas they are involved in and present them in such a way that people understand.", "They break things down using simple language that everyone comprehends. They recognise it doesn’t need to be rocket science.", "They observe people they talk to, seeing how they respond, and ask lots of questions, just to make sure that they have understood.", "They have a natural tendency to teach others. They enjoy sharing topics and interests with others through their explanations."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders lead with clarity and are adept at explaining a complex subject so that everyone understands and can take the relevant action. Leaders connect with their audience."]], ["In others", ["Help them to see when it is appropriate to include more detailed information. They may need to deliver complex messages that include high levels of detail."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may come across as patronising or oversimplifying things, rather than letting people work things out for themselves and learn as they go."]], ["Coaching questions", ["In what situations are you most effective at explaining concepts and information?", "How do you use your Explainer skill within your projects?", "What impact does your ability to simplify things have on others you work with?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Find ways to vary the audiences when giving an explanation, as a way to go from comfort to stretch zone. Try more senior, more diverse or larger groups, and reflect on any learnings after meetings.", "Be tuned in to the audience when sharing something. Note when others look engaged, tired, bored or confused, and act accordingly. Be mindful to know when to take a break and check in for understanding.", "Try new ways to explain things through videos, images and demonstrations. Ask for feedback on what the audience liked best and anything that didn’t work so well."]], ["In your teams", ["Use a variety of ways to engage the audience and adapt the team’s approach, according to the level of knowledge and experience their audience have. Consider video, seminars, meetings and one-to-ones."]]]}, {"s": "feedback", "n": "Feedback", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Feedback enjoy giving people both positive and negative feedback. They believe that it is important for people to know what they have done well, so that they can build on it and progress. Equally, they let people know where they can improve, delivering their feedback accurately, fairly and constructively.", "sec": [["At its best", ["People appreciate the fact that they are always open and honest with them.", "They feel compelled to ‘have a quiet word’ with people so they know whether they are on the right track, or if they should try something different.", "When they are giving someone constructive feedback, they try to impart any knowledge they have to help them with the learning.", "They enjoy evaluating and commenting on other people’s actions and decisions, so that they can see the impact they have, or could have, in the future."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love to be the person who seeks feedback on their interactions, the way they work and their performance. This enables them to learn quickly and grow as a leader."]], ["In others", ["Help them to support their feedback with reliable data and research, to ensure they aren’t depending too much on their personal views."]], ["Hidden risks", ["There is a time and a place for feedback. They may provide it at inappropriate times or contexts or offer it when it isn’t welcome."]], ["Coaching questions", ["When has your use of feedback made a real difference to a situation or someone?", "What enables you to be successful at giving feedback?", "How do you personally respond to feedback?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Consider the feedback that’s given to others. Don’t sandwich constructive feedback in between positive feedback – the positive will be forgotten. Stick to the point and give evidence so it doesn’t feel like an opinion.", "Lead the way for feedback at work. Encourage a culture where, at the end of the day or in a meeting, people pause and ask how others found it and what would make it better next time. Embrace the learning as a team.", "Actively invite feedback on work produced as well as feeding back on the work of others. Consider different people and perspectives to avoid asking the same reliable person each time."]], ["In your teams", ["Try to give others the opportunity to review their own work before the team give them feedback. This will give them the opportunity to monitor their own performance and development needs."]]]}, {"s": "gratitude", "n": "Gratitude", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Gratitude are constantly aware of how fortunate they are. They have a natural inclination to notice and appreciate the good things that happen to them. They don’t take anything or anyone for granted. Each day, they feel privileged to be who they are, being grateful for the life they lead and the positive things within it.", "sec": [["At its best", ["If something is going really well, however small, they tend to have a little sigh of contentment to themselves.", "They love it when everyone is happy working or getting on well. They tend to point out subtle things like this to others to give them a little lift during the day.", "They like to thank, praise and compliment – often when people least expect it.", "They enjoy taking time-out moments during the day, just to be thankful when things go well. They love to capture those moments."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders appreciate all the opportunities they’ve been given to reach their current position. They are grateful for the mentors and role models who’ve supported their leadership development."]], ["In others", ["Support them to see when the best time is to show gratitude and in what format, as well as tailor their approach for each individual."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may be sensitive to the lack of gratitude in other people for tasks and projects well received, rather than accepting a job well-done themselves."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What has taught you to be so appreciative for the good things in life?", "How do you use your Gratitude skill during your working day?", "What benefits does your skill in Gratitude bring to you and to others?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Bring appreciation into challenging situations and be a calming influence. Focus others on where they have come from and what they can achieve together from the learnings ahead, rather than dwell on the here and now.", "Create processes in teams to make gratitude more natural and a team effort. Have a channel dedicated to sharing acknowledgement and thanks, and ask everyone to contribute one post a week.", "Role model actively showing appreciation to others. This may be publicly in meetings or through informal gestures, such as a follow-up email, catch-up meeting or coffee. Make it genuine by sharing the impact they or their work had."]], ["In your teams", ["Keep the team’s level of gratitude proportionate to the cause. If they appear to be going overboard with gratitude, then they may not appear genuine, and the individual may go unappreciated."]]]}, {"s": "growth", "n": "Growth", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Growth actively seek out activities that will help them to develop, whether they are new skills, new knowledge, or a different way of doing things. They love to invite feedback on their performance, taking on board both positive and negative comments that will help them improve their performance and develop as a person.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They feel as though they are successful when learning a new topic. They try to learn something new every day.", "Even within subjects they know about, they delve deeper to find new angles, concepts, research and ideas to add to their learning.", "They are always looking to see if there are different ways that they can develop and improve their skills, no matter what they are doing.", "They love the whole experience of learning. The process of acquiring new skills and knowledge and getting feedback is brilliant."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders thrive in situations where they’re learning a great deal regularly, especially when fuelled by lots of feedback from peers and stakeholders."]], ["In others", ["Help them to find the balance between personal development and when it is best to collaborate on tasks and enjoy team growth."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may appear a little self-absorbed rather than finding a balance that considers other people’s needs and interests."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How does your Growth skill help you achieve your goals?", "What do you love about developing new skills and knowledge?", "How do you balance your appetite for growth with meeting your existing objectives?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Vary learning styles and acquire new skills in different ways. Try on-the-job learning, videos, reading, sharing with others, and online and classroom learning. Note preferences and what is most effective.", "Find ways to get outside a comfort zone in areas of motivation. Consider new or risky growth areas to explore that may have been shied away from to date. Which skills will be helpful?", "Ask for regular feedback on performance. Reflect on learning preferences and future career goals, to help focus on the right growth path. Re-visit the development plan regularly to see if it still supports priorities."]], ["In your teams", ["Set the team longer-term challenging targets. This will push them further, ensuring they focus on one or two important goals, rather than constantly looking for the next area of growth."]]]}, {"s": "humility", "n": "Humility", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Humility are humble, never allowing themselves to be big-headed or boastful. Although they feel satisfaction with a job well done, they tend to give other people credit for their successes rather than take the credit themselves. They know how much they depend on others for achieving things, and they appreciate and value their support.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They feel very honoured when they are part of a successful team. They enjoy the pleasure of working with some really bright and talented people.", "When working with others, they know that their achievements are down to everyone’s hard work and commitment, and that their role is often just a small part.", "When people congratulate them, they feel a bit uncomfortable. They are quick to point out that a project was such a success because of other people’s hard work.", "They know within themselves that what they contribute is valuable, but they don’t think that they are better than anyone else."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love acting immediately to support their own goals, initiatives and ideas. They know what to do and who to call on. If they don’t, they know that a way will be found to get started."]], ["In others", ["Support others to take more time to reflect on all possible options. Ask them to explain their chosen course of action so everyone’s clear, that way they can get the buy-in they need."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They can find the planning stage frustrating or are quickly bored when working with others who work slowly."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What energises you most about getting started and acting immediately?", "In what situations does your desire to act quickly reveal itself most strongly?", "How do you deal with situations where you can’t act quickly or where others may procrastinate?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Be factual and authentic when pointing out the contributions of others. Recognise the individual and team skills used, the mix of people in the team, and the difference the outcome has made to the organisation.", "Work with those who seem to lack confidence. Let others know how they contribute to the team or group, and the positive impact this has. Pay attention to the subtle wins that are often forgotten to highlight all the small things that contribute to success.", "Don’t be too modest when celebrating project outcomes. Raise awareness of absolutely everyone’s achievements, as well as the projects they were collaboratively involved in."]], ["In your teams", ["Ensure teams are focused on being busy on the right things. Visit the organisation’s strategy and goals regularly, to make sure they are on track with what is required."]]]}, {"s": "humour", "n": "Humour", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Humour love making people laugh and look for every opportunity to do it. They are able to see the funny side of things and like to share this with others. They can crack a joke, or tell a story that lightens the mood, gives enjoyment to others and helps people relax.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They overcome their fears by making light of them, turning them into something funny for everyone to laugh at.", "They are able to put people at ease with their Humour skill. They have a talent for easing tense situations.", "Whatever they are doing, and whoever they are doing it with, they are always trying to cheer people up.", "They can make any situation seem funny – they just love making people laugh."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love being the person who can make light of situations. Others can rely on them to put people at ease and lighten the mood quickly, often by laughing authentically at themselves."]], ["In others", ["Help others build their ability to recognise when humour is appropriate and when to hold back, or others may not take them seriously."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may fail to recognise when humour is appropriate or welcome, causing offense or to limit their ability to seem professional when required."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What do you love most about making people laugh?", "What do you achieve through using humour at work?", "When necessary, how do you dial down your strength in Humour?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Use humour to naturally engage with others. Get to know different audiences and dial humour up and down when presenting to make explanations or dull topics more memorable.", "Focus humour at work and use it wisely to help defuse tension or address a difficult situation. A well-timed joke can reduce stress in the moment and boost morale.", "Laughter has a positive effect on others, so encourage it appropriately. Arrange a fun social and develop authentic relationships with a positive team culture."]], ["In your teams", ["There may be times when it is necessary to focus on important messages seriously or deal with challenging situations. Encourage team members to speak up if they feel uncomfortable or frustrated."]]]}, {"s": "implementer", "n": "Implementer", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People strong in Implementer get great satisfaction bridging the gap between an abstract concept and an efficient solution. They have a pragmatic approach to ensure the implementation of a cost-effective solution for any project or initiative. They play a crucial role in making sure ideas are readily adoptable.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They naturally collaborate effectively with stakeholders and team members to align on goals, priorities and implementation strategies.", "They enjoy approaching problems and projects with a practical mindset, focusing on finding solutions that are feasible, cost-effective and actionable.", "They love to be proactive and action-oriented, taking initiative to drive the implementation process forward.", "They prioritise taking action over engaging in theoretical debates, diving straight into turning ideas into actionable solutions that are readily adoptable."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Implementer will be keen to take action to bring ideas to fruition. They will encourage their teams to consider practical strategies for execution of goals and projects."]], ["In others", ["Support others to balance their practical mindset with taking time to research the best solutions and collaborating with others. This will ensure they have a clear understanding of the objectives."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may sometimes make assumptions about what is to be implemented. Ask them to share their understanding of this with colleagues, to check that it is a workable solution."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What has been your most successful project where you initiated a practical solution to an idea?", "What excites you about making something real and feasible?", "Who typically benefits from your easy to apply approaches to problems?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Offer to get more involved in budget management for projects. Identify opportunities to introduce cost savings without sacrificing quality, and ensure that implementation projects remain on schedule and meet required deadlines.", "Learn about change management strategies and techniques to facilitate the adoption of new ideas and solutions within the organisation. Understand the culture being worked in, anticipate resistance to change and develop plans to address it proactively.", "Endeavour to bridge the gap between abstract concepts and efficient solutions that meet the priorities of the business. Ensure that ideas are easily adoptable, and research and contribute to the success of projects and initiatives."]], ["In your teams", ["A bias towards action and implementation may lead to overlooking alternative or new perspectives. Regularly challenge those in the team to consider multiple approaches, weighing pros and cons of each."]]]}, {"s": "improver", "n": "Improver", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Improver get a thrill from seeing how they can make things more effective. They love looking for ways to do things better, even when they are not asked to! They have an innate sense of how to improve on ways of doing things for themselves and others.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They believe that anything can be done better, and they just need to review something with fresh eyes and have done their research.", "They often have a reputation for being good with processes. Technology is constantly changing, so there are always new ways to approach a task.", "They love to take something that isn’t working as well as it could and make it simpler, clearer, more logical, more efficient or simply better.", "They never have to be told when to improve something – even if it isn’t something they are working on. They just naturally do it."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders get excited when they see opportunities to improve the effectiveness and efficiency of something and are energised when making improvements."]], ["In others", ["Help them to be mindful of when it may be best to leave things as they are. Guide them on when to improve things, and when working methods are fine as they are."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may find it hard to stop adjusting and tinkering with things, knowing there are always improvements, when there are other more pressing priorities."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What drives you to look for better ways of doing things?", "When have you used your Improver skill to achieve an outcome you are proud of?", "How do you judge when it’s suitable to leave things as they are?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Volunteer for additional tasks or projects that provide opportunities for learning, growth, and developing new and improved ways of working. Collaborate cross-functionally to share improvement ideas for current organisational challenges.", "Measure the success of improvements before moving on to the next thing. There may be short- and long-term gains. Use this as the business case for the next idea.", "Be conscious of making considered recommendations. Do the research on cost, time, impact and any risks before presenting ideas to senior stakeholders."]], ["In your teams", ["Watch out for improving something without the strategic view. Consider the real added value and whether you are working on time consuming short-term fixes rather than looking at the bigger picture."]]]}, {"s": "incisive", "n": "Incisive", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People strong in Incisive have the innate ability to cut through the noise and understand the essential parts to issues or situations. They can rapidly identify the key aspects of intricate scenarios, meaning others count on their precision.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They naturally seem to understand the root of the problem and come up with the essential components to move problems forward.", "They enjoy breaking down complex problems or situations into their essential components to gain a deeper understanding of what the core issue is.", "They engage in critical thinking and questioning to dissect complex issues and identify the underlying factors or root causes.", "They offer clear and insightful perspectives on complicated situations, making them a valuable resource for gaining clarity and direction."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Incisive will help their teams to get to the core of any situation and avoid being distracted by irrelevant information. They will provide essential guidance when teams feel pulled in many directions."]], ["In others", ["Support colleagues to balance their ability to share the essential point of complex issues with that of taking time to explain the cause and more detail of important matters. This will support learning among the team."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may presume that others are only interested in the pertinent points about an important issue. At times, they may be better off giving people a more informed breakdown of the problem."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What is your process for reviewing something and understanding what needs to be done?", "What’s a recent issue you were key to supporting with your precision?", "What helps you to be confident in your findings, particularly when working quickly?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Look for opportunities to offer constructive and precise solutions to complex situations or challenges that clients are experiencing. Help stakeholders be decisive with the information available.", "Reflect on a problem from different perspectives. Speak with others and ask probing questions to understand how they would interpret it. Use these different viewpoints to find a well-informed conclusion.", "Be mindful about jumping to conclusions. Take time to break down a problem and understand or challenge common assumptions. Seek clarity and look for evidence to support or refute those conclusions."]], ["In your teams", ["Quick decision-making may not feel thorough to the team, if some value collaboration and deliberation. Spend extra time ensuring each member of the team has had a chance to voice their input."]]]}, {"s": "inclusion", "n": "Inclusion", "f": "Relating", "x": "People strong in Inclusion are able to draw immense satisfaction from fostering inclusivity within activities and projects, ensuring that everyone feels part of the team. Their proactive approach encourages participation from all individuals, regardless of their background or expertise.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They strongly believe in inclusion and help colleagues they meet to feel a sense of belonging in their environment and part of the wider organisation.", "They are known for understanding and appreciating other people’s unique backgrounds, perspectives and challenges, encouraging colleagues to share their uniqueness with others.", "They love ensuring that activities and projects are accessible to all team members, accommodating diverse needs and preferences.", "They like to encourage participation from everyone in their team, to draw on differing perspectives and drive success."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Inclusion love to be part of their teams and bring people together to appreciate different interests and unique experiences. They know that we can all learn from each other, and they encourage this in their interactions."]], ["In others", ["Support others to be aware when it is necessary to include everyone or when it is more important to have a select group. It may not be appropriate to include everyone in time-sensitive decision-making."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may feel that everyone must be included in meetings at all times. To manage this costly risk, ensure that only key stakeholders are invited and share notes to a wider group afterwards when appropriate."]], ["Coaching questions", ["In what ways do you find yourself actively ensuring people feel part of groups?", "What do you see as the benefits for the organisation when people feel a sense of belonging?", "How does it make you feel when you help everyone in the group find their voice and be heard?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Make a conscious effort to learn more about different cultures, backgrounds and perspectives. Understanding and appreciating these differences will enable people to create inclusive environments where everyone feels valued and respected.", "Encourage participation and collaboration from all team members, even if it is not their subject area. Create opportunities for everyone to contribute their ideas and opinions in a supportive environment and embrace collaborative decision-making processes that involve input from diverse perspectives.", "Reflect on business practices in the organisation to identify areas where a more diverse range of colleagues can be asked to contribute. Seek the opinion of others and be open to constructive feedback about how to create more inclusive environments."]], ["In your teams", ["Champion inclusion when it comes to innovation and implementing new ideas. Be mindful, however, of other people’s time and involve individuals where their contributions will be most valuable."]]]}, {"s": "incubator", "n": "Incubator", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Incubator love to think, to ponder, to reflect. They like to take moments out of their day, week or month to give themselves dedicated thinking time, to think deeply about things. These times are very precious to them, allowing them the time and space to be absorbed in their own thoughts, without annoying distractions.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They work best when they have the chance to mull something over, rather than needing to make instant decisions.", "When they talk to people, they need to give themselves time to reflect on what and how something was said, and then bounce it around in their head for a bit.", "Decision-making takes them longer than others as they must think things through, but they are then able to come up with the right way forward.", "They enjoy giving themselves time to hold something in their mind for a while and not consciously work on it, as the answer often then just pops out."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders take time to process information and reflect on decisions so that outcomes are thoroughly considered. This gives them more confidence about the decisions they make."]], ["In others", ["Help them to avoid being seen as slow or unresponsive. Ask them to share their preferred style with others while ensuring they know when a quick decision is necessary."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They should be careful not to ponder everything or they could come across as passive and avoiding the quicker or more decisive approach that’s necessary."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do you ensure you make time to think things through when you are busy?", "What drives you to give a lot of thought to things or important decisions?", "What impact do your reflecting skills have on others?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Strive to be organised where possible, in order to do important thinking in advance of meetings. Read up on the subject or ask for an agenda before a meeting, to be able to contribute fully in it.", "Limit distractions for set periods of the working day, to focus on thinking time, and try blocking out time in the diary. Remember, having time to process ideas and concepts is essential, not a luxury.", "Try to partner with those with skills in Action, Counterpoint or Initiative. Quicker decisions when a deadline is imminent will be made, along with ideas to stimulate thinking."]], ["In your teams", ["Protect the team’s thinking time at the start of longer-term or complex projects to ensure all angles are covered. That way, they can move forward confidently, without continually needing to look back."]]]}, {"s": "initiative", "n": "Initiative", "f": "Relating", "x": "People strong in Initiative relish the opportunity to take the lead and move projects forward. They love to make decisions autonomously to ensure tasks are started. They naturally assume a proactive role and guide others in times of uncertainty.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They find joy in making swift decisions and getting things started on a new project.", "They naturally seize control of something that needs a leading role and thrive at executing tasks in times of uncertainty.", "They are confident to be proactive in a variety of situations. They relish it when team members come to them before embarking on something.", "They like working on their own and having the ability to make choices that can move tasks forward and get colleagues involved at the right time."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Initiative naturally react under pressure by taking the lead and making the decision that gets projects started, giving others confidence to follow them. Stakeholders know if they ask them for something, it will get started."]], ["In others", ["Support others to share the rationale behind new ventures they’re launching with colleagues. Team members will be on board if they can see that the initiative is well informed and supports the organisation’s objectives."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may take the initiative to get started on a project before they have all the available information. Remind them to take a pause to assess whether they need more data before proceeding on a project."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What projects in particular do you love to take the initiative on?", "Do others look to you for getting something started? Why do you think this is?", "Have you ever made the wrong decision on an initiative? What did you learn?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Adopt a more strategic vision, understanding the broader goals and objectives of the organisation’s projects. This will help the development of better-informed resource decisions when leading a new or innovative initiative.", "Enhance skills in setting clear and achievable goals and how to articulate these in a way that gets buy-in from others. Creating detailed action plans outlining the steps needed to accomplish objectives will ensure team members can take over the lead from you confidently.", "Learn to anticipate potential risks and develop contingency plans to mitigate them. Meet with team members to understand any threats or challenges regularly. Proactively address challenges that arise during project execution, ensuring that progress is not hindered."]], ["In your teams", ["Do a select few regularly volunteer to take on work or projects? One person’s eagerness to take charge may take away opportunities for others to contribute. Encourage a team effort on new ideas."]]]}, {"s": "innovation", "n": "Innovation", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Innovation want to be the first to design and create things that are new and have never been thought of. They can look at things from a different perspective to others, thinking laterally and ‘out of the box’. Their ideas and inventions help move things forward.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They don’t get blinkered by what exists now but look beyond this and into the realms of what could be.", "Nothing gets them more excited than someone saying “I don’t think that can be done”. They are often finding out more before they’ve finished the sentence.", "They are always trying out things from scratch, coming up with ideas that have never been tried before, and seeing what they can do to get them to work.", "They’ve usually got a few ideas bubbling around in their head that haven’t come to fruition yet. They just enjoy having them there."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are in their element producing new ways to solve problems and implementing solutions that others haven’t. People value this quality within their leadership."]], ["In others", ["Help others to see when there is merit in existing ways of working that are effective. Discuss the benefits of tried-and-tested solutions and when innovation is appropriate."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may innovate for the sake of it, coming across as obstructive or wanting to delay a project. They may need to learn there is a time and a place for lateral thinking."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What’s your proudest innovative moment?", "What drives you to come up with new ways of doing things?", "When do you come up with your ideas for innovative solutions?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Spend time with other innovative colleagues to bounce novel ideas around, and with team members strong in organising to help the suggestions come to fruition.", "Is a business process no longer as beneficial as it once was? Share thoughts with the relevant people about new advancements in technology, and support any arguments about the positive impact of a new approach over the existing business process.", "Look for opportunities to present innovative ideas to clients. Run a session to understand their challenges, direct new concepts, and explore which are viable and which will add most value to them."]], ["In your teams", ["Don’t ignore the past. The team may be able to learn by looking back at what has been done before, identifying ways to improve this and take it even further with their innovative suggestions."]]]}, {"s": "judgement", "n": "Judgement", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Judgement make decisions quickly and easily. They enjoy quickly assessing the evidence of any situation. They are confident that they will reach the right decision through their rapid analysis and weighing up the facts. They take pride in the fact that their decisions stand up to robust challenge, and usually turn out to be right.", "sec": [["At its best", ["Making decisions just seems to come naturally to them. As well as weighing up the facts, they apply their experience and perspective.", "To others, it might look like they sometimes rush decisions, but they have all the important points uppermost in their mind.", "People come to them all the time asking for their advice, often in subjects they are really not very experienced in.", "They like to look at a situation and list the pros and cons in their head before deciding. It doesn’t take long."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love being the person who others can rely on to assess the situation and reach a conclusion that they can easily justify their reasoning for and will stick to."]], ["In others", ["Help others to focus decision-making on analysis so they can see the value of facts and opinions before moving ahead with their decisions."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may fail to consider wider perspectives by not making time to seek and incorporate the views and opinions of others."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What enables and guides you to make the right decision quickly and easily?", "How do others value your decision-making ability?", "In what situations does your skill in Judgement work best?"]], ["Upskilling", ["In a situation where any reasoning and benefits may seem perfectly clear, take time to explain the rationale to clients and stakeholders, in order to evidence decisions and get their support.", "Put together a checklist of things to consider when making decisions. Consider milestones, stakeholders, processes, challenges and benefits to be able to make informed decisions.", "Talk through swifter or more important decisions before implementing them, so others can understand the view and reasoning and to get their buy-in. Involve team members in the decision-making where possible."]], ["In your teams", ["As a team, ensure they spend enough time considering all available information before making a decision. Did they weigh up all their options, or just most of them?"]]]}, {"s": "learning-agility", "n": "Learning Agility", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People strong in Learning Agility have an innate ability to absorb information and learning quickly, swiftly applying this new-found knowledge. Their natural confidence to grasp and learn new things very rapidly makes them highly proficient in many situations.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They take every opportunity to learn from their own and other people’s experience, as they are keen to improve their future performance.", "They have the ability to quickly grasp and understand complex concepts and information, enabling them to learn rapidly and share their knowledge with others.", "They are resourceful in seeking out learning opportunities and leveraging available resources, such as mentors, courses and online materials, to facilitate their learning journey.", "They are known throughout the organisation for becoming the expert in a wide range of subjects in a short amount of time."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Learning Agility enjoy applying their frequent leadership learnings or knowledge gained from observing industry trends. They know there’s no point acquiring new skills and knowledge if they don’t use them to develop their leadership style."]], ["In others", ["Support others to share their new knowledge and application approaches with colleagues to further the team’s efficiencies while being mindful that team members may not learn as fast."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may want to get on with applying their new learnings without considering the wider impact on whether it is the best approach. Invite them to assess whether an existing process is more appropriate than applying what they’ve recently learned."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What was the last piece of learning you undertook that you were able to use practically?", "What types of topics and information do you typically gravitate towards when learning?", "How do you ensure you are able to apply your learning?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Set aside time each week to actively learn new things. Establish clear developmental goals that align with interests and career aspirations. Endeavour to make learning a lifelong habit.", "Identify areas where learning has been challenging. Seek guidance and mentorship from experienced individuals in this area, in order to learn from their insights and experiences to accelerate growth. Try to align learning with motivation.", "Adopt a learning and growth mindset, believing that abilities and skills can be developed through effort and learning. Engage in activities or tasks that stretch beyond current skill levels and use possible setbacks as learning opportunities."]], ["In your teams", ["Some people naturally learn and apply concepts quicker than others. Those who are quick to learn should find opportunities to distil the concepts acquired to make it easier for others to learn."]]]}, {"s": "legacy", "n": "Legacy", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Legacy care deeply about future generations and seek to leave a legacy through what they do. They enjoy working on things that make a difference and will have a positive impact on others. In whatever they do, they want to ensure that they create something that will outlast them and continue to make a positive contribution after they’ve gone.", "sec": [["At its best", ["It is their goal that their work will live on without them. What they leave behind is important to them.", "They firmly believe that we should all leave the world a better place than when we entered it.", "In everything they do, they think about the impact it will have on the people who follow them. Others, in turn, can improve on this and impact future generations.", "They believe there is so much we can do to make a difference. They love to learn from people who have left behind their ideas in books and quotes."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are passionate about having a long-lasting influence with whatever projects they or their teams are involved in. This includes reviewing the impact they’ll all have well into the future."]], ["In others", ["Help others balance focusing on the future with enjoying what’s happening around them, to avoid missing out on opportunities to make an immediate impact."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may miss out on opportunities to celebrate or appreciate what’s happened that day or week which could mean they come across as dismissive."]], ["Coaching questions", ["In what ways do you want to leave a lasting positive impact on future generations?", "How does your Legacy skill affect your daily decisions at work?", "How do you currently satisfy your Legacy skill?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Consider roles where there are opportunities to influence the longer-term outcomes for the organisation. Help each team member visualise the impact they could personally have by trying something new or changing an existing approach.", "Understand the different causes that stakeholders are passionate about and why. Seek inspiration from them, finding ways to be motivated in supporting and adding value to their causes.", "What lasting difference could be made to the organisation’s services or products? Is there something that could be started, changed or stopped that would have a positive and lasting impact for clients?"]], ["In your teams", ["Don’t be afraid to try out new projects as a team. They may not be guaranteed to be long-lasting, but you never know. Consider smaller ways to change the shape of things too."]]]}, {"s": "listener", "n": "Listener", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Listener show a keen interest in what people have to say and the way that they say it. When people are speaking to them, they focus intently on them and what they are saying. They listen to not only the words but also how those words are used. Everything that someone says is important – they don’t miss a thing.", "sec": [["At its best", ["Listening closely to someone is a big compliment to them. Everyone loves to be listened to and they love to do that for people.", "They find everyone interesting. They enjoy listening to people’s stories as they are all fascinating to them.", "When they listen to people, they focus completely on the person talking so that they know they are listening. They shut out the entire world.", "Their colleagues love to seek them out and tell them about their problems. They are always there for others, and they enjoy it."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are genuinely interested in people and what they have to say. They love being fully present and in the moment so they can understand exactly what they need to do or say to support someone."]], ["In others", ["Encourage them to both listen and speak. It may be helpful to be more active in some conversations, to demonstrate the outcomes of their listening skills."]], ["Hidden risks", ["Their skill in showing a keen interest in what others have to say could see them come across as passive or lacking in contributions during meetings."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What is most enjoyable for you when listening intently to others?", "How does the Listener skill help you most in your work activities?", "How do you judge when to engage in conversation, rather than listen?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Find ways of listening to a variety of peers, so as to support and learn from a diverse group rather than always from the same people. Choose people with different backgrounds and experiences and at varying levels in the organisation.", "Avoid offering a solution too quickly and instead fully listen to a client’s needs. Reflect back to the client what was heard and understood, and give them reassurance that they have been listened to and will receive the help needed.", "Offer to be a sounding board to colleagues for them to talk through challenges they may be experiencing at work. Sometimes talking and having someone listen can lead colleagues to come up with the solution themselves."]], ["In your teams", ["While it is important to listen to what people say, make sure everyone contributes to a discussion. It may be necessary to demonstrate that the team understand what they have heard."]]]}, {"s": "mission", "n": "Mission", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Mission derive great fulfilment from pursuing activities that give their life meaning and purpose. The focus of their purpose could be one or many different things, but whatever it is, they are committed to pursuing it. How they spend their time, the decisions they make, and the plans they have for the future are all aligned with their overriding sense of mission and purpose.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They try to follow their heart and do things that are worthwhile, giving a real sense of meaning to people’s lives.", "They are committed to working for the benefit of others, to making their lives more enjoyable, and to making this sustainable.", "Making a difference is at the heart of everything they do at work and at home.", "Working for the right organisation is far more important than their pay or benefits. They need to do what matters, because nothing else does."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are committed to improving the lives of others and doing important work. They love to influence others to back their aspirations, getting each to play their part and feel part of the mission."]], ["In others", ["Support them to understand how smaller projects contribute to the organisation’s overall mission as well so that they don’t avoid what might seem like unimportant tasks."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may let their mission be the only mission and dominate everything they do, rather than flex their style and know when to contribute to helping others achieve their own life goals."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What do you want to focus on at work when it comes to making a difference?", "What drives you towards a life of mission and purpose?", "How to you get buy-in from others to support your mission?"]], ["Upskilling", ["At the start of a project, meet with the key stakeholder to understand the purpose and outcomes. Use these reasons as an incentive to be motivated to deliver only the best work in a way that positively impacts the customer, team and organisation.", "Review current personal or team goals. Look to partner with those strong in Organiser or Action, or draw on strength in Personal Responsibility, to make sure the mission is realised through each task and project.", "Be clear with the business case of a mission. Discuss with team members the facts and share a strong purpose for this work. Opinions don’t count when persuading others, so focus more on providing meaning and purpose to motivate them."]], ["In your teams", ["What do the team know about the individual goals of team members? How could they contribute to each other’s mission, as well as working on their own and the team’s?"]]]}, {"s": "moral-compass", "n": "Moral Compass", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Moral Compass are very moral and have an extremely strong ethical code. They are very aware of the difference between right and wrong, and always act in accordance with what they believe is right. They are clear on their values and their moral reasoning for what they do and why they do it.", "sec": [["At its best", ["Their decisions and actions are always guided by their ethics and values, and they never step outside of these.", "They just have to do the right thing. They always stand by this and believe it’s the only way to make decisions that affect people.", "Their personal position becomes irrelevant if there are consequences from them doing the right thing.", "Their colleagues know where they stand with them as they are very faithful and consistent. They have a clear view of what is acceptable."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders passionately lead in a way that is congruent with their beliefs. They role model working by their principles to their teams so that the teams see the behaviours they expect them to replicate."]], ["In others", ["Help them to accept views that oppose their own when it comes to important decision-making. Support them to compromise and understand the bigger picture."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may come across as a bit righteous or judge others strictly by their own standards, rather than learning what is important to those around them."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does your skill of acting morally come from?", "How does your Moral Compass skill guide your day-to-day actions and decisions?", "Which moral action or decision brings you most pride?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Connect with people who share similar values at work. Join forces for a greater impact and voice on important issues that will support the organisation’s values.", "Share strong values with others so they understand any challenges ahead with moral decisions. Be consistent in upholding these values and transparent in any reasoning so others respect them.", "Look at exploring and developing personal views and ethical codes. Discuss with others what is felt to be the ethical course of action to take on new initiatives and why this is. Challenge any approaches with evidence, not just opinion."]], ["In your teams", ["Appreciate that team members may have different moral codes. Come up with a compromise that the whole team can agree with and follow that is aligned with the wider business values."]]]}, {"s": "narrator", "n": "Narrator", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Narrator have a tremendous love of story. Even as ordinary events happen, they can picture how they might be spun into an anecdote or story for them to tell others. They love to answer questions in the form of a story. They can always see the power of stories to convey morals, insights, values, humour and other lessons to people.", "sec": [["At its best", ["For them, life is one big story waiting to be told. It comes so naturally to them, they don’t even notice they are telling a story.", "They often use stories as a way of relating to people. They encourage people to share their own, so they can store them up to tell others later.", "They find they can engage an audience easily as they take a concept or fact and then take people on a journey of its importance.", "They are always using their own experiences to make people laugh. It works much better than a joke and people remember the point of the story."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are adept at turning their everyday experiences into powerful anecdotes and stories for others. This helps them create a narrative for themselves as a leader that is meaningful and purposeful."]], ["In others", ["Support them to see when a short response is perfectly adequate. Help them to recognise that long responses could become tiresome for people who prefer concise information."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may use stories and anecdotes to relate everyday events to the point of being wearing for others, rather than learning when a direct or factual response is appropriate."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What was the last effective story you told to deliver an important message?", "What approaches do you take in creating stories?", "In what situations does your storytelling work best with others?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Adapt stories for the audience and the outcome needed. Consider their background, experiences, seniority and interests to find the right approach that resonates.", "Team up with a colleague with the Writer skill and consider the variety of ways to tell a story. Try social media, blogs, a case study or a graphic to take a break from a long verbal or written performance.", "Share professional experiences and insights with team members, contributing authentic stories they can relate to. Invite others to then ask questions so they can be given a detailed elaboration."]], ["In your teams", ["Manage team meetings effectively. Give everyone in the team the chance to speak but keep an eye on the time spent telling stories rather than getting to the point."]]]}, {"s": "opportunity-spotter", "n": "Opportunity Spotter", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People strong in Opportunity Spotter have an inherent ability to spot and seize opportunities and know how to use them to their advantage. They actively seek ways to enhance and add value to situations, benefitting projects and situations in the short or long term.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They possess a creative mindset that allows them to think creatively and come up with solutions to capitalise on opportunities in unique and unexpected ways.", "They are active in building their connections and seek ways to leverage their networks’ knowledge to uncover new opportunities.", "They are highly observant and attuned to their surroundings, constantly scanning for potential opportunities and how they can take advantage of them.", "They are known for seeing possibilities where others may see obstacles, generating new ideas and solutions to capitalise on opportunities to benefit the organisation."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders with a strength in Opportunity Spotter love to be curious, ask questions and be open to opportunities. They know there is always something to discover about how they lead and how their teams work. They encourage team members to seek out new opportunities for development."]], ["In others", ["Support others to reflect on whether a new opportunity should be explored when working on an existing task. They may need to be reminded to finish a current task before moving on to another project."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may spend too much time looking for the next opportunity to win new business. Remind them to ensure that they spend time focusing on the needs of existing and recently won clients."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What was the last opportunity you spotted? What happened?", "What areas of business or knowledge do you typically find opportunities in?", "What is your approach once you have seen an opportunity to apply it?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Stay informed about current trends, competitor developments and emerging technologies relevant to an organisation’s goals or interests. Read wide-ranging publications and attend diverse events to stay ahead of the curve.", "Embrace calculated risks in pursuit of promising opportunities. Assess the potential rewards and consequences of different options and be prepared to take action when the potential benefits outweigh the risks. Evaluate any uncertain projects quickly and adapt the approach if necessary.", "Actively network with a diverse group of individuals, across a range of backgrounds, fields and industries. Building a broad network increases exposure to a wider array of opportunities and perspectives."]], ["In your teams", ["The team may struggle to see things through to completion. Meet as a team regularly to reflect on this and escalate or deprioritise as needed to balance starting new projects with closing out existing ones."]]]}, {"s": "optimism", "n": "Optimism", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Optimism always see the best side of any situation, with a consistent and firm belief that things will work out well in the future. Having this belief keeps them strong and enables them to stay positive, even when things are difficult.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They always look on the bright side of life. They just hate being miserable.", "They often find that people around them complain about things more than they do. They don’t see the point – they believe you have to make the best of the situation you’re in.", "They usually bring everyone else’s positive spirits up, as they are told their positivity can be contagious.", "In life, there will always be challenges, so it’s important to think positively – and that’s exactly what they always do."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are a source of encouragement and inspiration to others around them, able to see the positive outcomes or potential upside in any situation presented."]], ["In others", ["Help them to understand when they may have unrealistic expectations of a situation or challenge. Support them to look beyond the bright side and address the issue."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may get frustrated with others who don’t want to feel lifted but simply need a safe place to work things out for themselves."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What enables you to maintain a positive outlook during challenges?", "What impact does being optimistic have on your work outcomes?", "How do you help bring others around to a positive way of viewing things?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Find ways to share with clients valid and factual reasons for hope and optimism to avoid coming across as naive. The reality combined with positivity will be very influential for choosing the best outcomes together.", "Volunteer for more difficult or longer-term projects to keep spirits high and people motivated. Help team members see how the learnings can be applied to improve growth and innovation for other projects or clients.", "Look for opportunities to be naturally optimistic in meetings. Discuss current projects and the practical ways issues have been solved. Help others to visualise success and keep things in perspective."]], ["In your teams", ["Ensure the team deliver the results they are so good at being positive about. Which organising skills can the team combine with their Optimism skill to ensure that deadlines are met?"]]]}, {"s": "orchestrator", "n": "Orchestrator", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People strong in Orchestrator get great satisfaction from co-ordinating diverse resources, ensuring their collective optimal performance. They innately match people who have the necessary expertise to ensure successful project outcomes. They assemble great teams to work productively together with the right resources.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are known for effectively considering people’s expertise and skills and matching them to specific tasks and roles within a project.", "They are team-oriented individuals who recognise the importance of teamwork and possess a deep understanding of how people work together effectively.", "They are excited when facilitating productive discussions and aligning diverse perspectives, so that stakeholders and clients are energised.", "They thrive in environments where they can structure and co-ordinate various tasks and resources, to ensure peak performance from colleagues."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Orchestrator skilfully know who to call on and exactly what for. They pride themselves on bringing together and leading effective teams whose results go beyond the scope of work and exceed stakeholders’ expectations."]], ["In others", ["Support others to avoid frustration when there is less flexibility in resources by encouraging them to adapt and find new ways forward on a project."]], ["Hidden risks", ["Sometimes they may get frustrated if needing to work with the resources they have, especially when they might not have the required skills. Encourage them to motivate others to go beyond their current capabilities."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do you ensure you successfully co-ordinate people and resources?", "How do you keep on top of bigger projects requiring many things to be working together?", "What types of projects do you prefer getting involved in when you are in a facilitation role?"]], ["Upskilling", ["At the outset of a project, meet with the stakeholder and outline the resources needed. Highlight the skills that will be required and suggest colleagues. Create a detailed plan with relevant milestones.", "Assemble diverse teams and get to know the complementary strengths and expertise of each team member. Cultivate strong partnerships with other teams and look at ways of working together that may be mutually beneficial.", "Develop skills in resource optimisation by giving due consideration to the resources needed, the timescales that may exist, and the budgetary constraints that must be adhered to. Prioritise tasks and allocate resources according to the organisation’s strategy to maximise efficiency and productivity."]], ["In your teams", ["Do team members feel they are simply getting volunteered for tasks? Allow room to discuss personal preferences and strengths to ensure each team member has appropriate autonomy in choosing their role in projects."]]]}, {"s": "organiser", "n": "Organiser", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Organiser love to be exceptionally well organised. Everything has its place, in both their work and their personal life. Their order and organisation mean that they are able to be as effective as possible in everything they do. They always know where things are, what to do when, and why something is important.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They firmly believe that everything has its place. When it doesn’t, nothing delights them more than to find a place for it.", "In all areas of their life, they like things to be organised. Knowing where everything is helps them focus on what they need to do.", "They get really excited when they are asked to organise something that is a complete mess. They find it so satisfying to see the end result.", "Their colleagues often rely on them for events and meetings, as they think of everything and everyone to make it successful."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are known for having everything in order. There’s a strong element of discipline and rigour to their leadership style that supports their speed and effectiveness."]], ["In others", ["Help them to see when they may appear too structured or demonstrate a lack of flexibility. Encourage them to consider when it is beneficial to go with the flow."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may get frustrated with the appearance of others being dis-organised or not be open to new ideas and ways of doing things."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What motivates you about being organised?", "How does being organised help you to achieve your daily tasks at work?", "In what situation have your organisational skills been the most effective?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Understand team preferences when it comes to organising, in order to create less friction in projects. Encourage people to compromise, allowing for more efficiency and the right level of structure for the task.", "Use others to help get organised when necessary or during time-sensitive projects. Borrow templates, delegate version controls and use tools to share the load. Not everyone needs to be the organiser in a project.", "Ask for a good level of structure in projects to assist further order. Seek clarity on timescales and break tasks down into actionable steps, avoiding too much organisation up front."]], ["In your teams", ["Appreciate that there will always be unexpected changes in the team. Build a degree of flexibility into projects and use those who are strong in Adaptable to champion the changes."]]]}, {"s": "pace", "n": "Pace", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People strong in Pace love to execute tasks swiftly and are confidently and professionally able to take rapid action. Their work is balanced effectively between speed and efficiency, producing high-quality outputs. They are naturally self-motivated and deliver the right outcomes quickly, even in the face of challenges.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They effectively manage their time, prioritising tasks based on their importance and deadlines, so that they can get started promptly.", "They maintain focus and concentration on the task at hand, minimising distractions and interruptions, in order to achieve their goals quickly.", "They are naturally self-motivated, driven by a strong desire to deliver results quickly and efficiently, impressing stakeholders along the way.", "They are constantly ensuring that important objectives are promptly addressed and completed without compromising quality."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders strong in Pace love picking up new projects and quickly gathering information and requirements so their teams can start as soon as possible. They like to create energy and direction from the outset and maintain this throughout projects."]], ["In others", ["Support others to consider the impact on colleagues of moving too quickly on projects. Others may need to ponder decisions first or go at a slower pace."]], ["Hidden risks", ["At times, they may move at too fast a pace for their colleagues to keep up with. Remind them to check in to see if others are on track or struggling with something that is slowing them down."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do you ensure accuracy when working at pace?", "What tactics help you to remain calm when having to work through something important at speed?", "What is it you prefer about working quickly rather than taking more time?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Develop skills in risk management to anticipate potential obstacles or pitfalls that may slow down progress on tasks. Proactively identify threats, assess their potential impact, and develop contingency plans to mitigate them and expedite project delivery.", "When completing a task becomes urgent, create an environment where there are minimal distractions. Turn off messaging apps and let colleagues know when is suitable to get in touch, so that outside this time, tasks can be worked on at pace.", "Working at pace may be best served by identifying opportunities to streamline processes and eliminate unnecessary steps that hinder efficiency. Look for ways to automate tasks by leveraging technology to speed up productivity."]], ["In your teams", ["Slower periods of work may feel de-energising when the team are used to projects that require working quickly and at pace. Use this time to focus on self-development and re-energise ahead of busy periods to avoid burnout."]]]}, {"s": "performance-focus", "n": "Performance Focus", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People strong in Performance Focus steer individuals towards achieving their goals and targets by emphasising the importance of delivering performance. They regularly articulate the objectives of their people and teams to ensure they are centre of attention to those around them.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They naturally help people focus on delivering the necessary results, bringing clarity to what needs to be done, so that colleagues are set up for success.", "They communicate regularly and effectively about performance expectations, objectives and progress towards goals, with the aim of team members performing optimally.", "They motivate and inspire others to strive for excellence and surpass expectations, so that they produce their best work.", "They constantly monitor and track progress towards performance goals and targets, so that they can see targets are on track."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders with a strength in Performance Focus like to hold others accountable for their performance. They are clear on their expectations to reinforce what needs to be done and they do everything they can to support their teams to achieve results."]], ["In others", ["Support others to focus on wellbeing as well as performance. Remind them that having a healthy work-life balance can avoid burnout in colleagues and enable them to perform better."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They might focus too strongly on delivering excellent performance, which may impact negatively on people’s wellbeing. Remind them to look out for signs they might be pushing others too hard to perform."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do you work with others to ensure you stay focused and on track?", "How do you keep motivation high with more challenging projects?", "What communication methods work well to bring consistency and clarity to your goals?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Set clear, achievable goals and ensure these are specific, measurable, attainable, relevant and time-bound (SMART). Regularly communicate them to keep colleagues focused. Ensure that individual and team goals are aligned with broader organisational objectives and priorities.", "Identify key performance indicators (KPIs) relevant to objectives and regularly track progress towards them. Establish benchmarks for success and use performance data to assess individual and team performance objectively.", "Provide timely and constructive feedback to team members, acknowledging achievements and addressing areas for improvement. Recognise and celebrate milestones and successes to reinforce performance-focused behaviours and encourage continued effort."]], ["In your teams", ["Operating with a performance-focused mentality may lead to the team using unsustainable methods, or undervaluing its people, to achieve an outcome. Take time to conduct retrospectives to understand what worked, and what didn’t, to improve the process the next time around."]]]}, {"s": "persistence", "n": "Persistence", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Persistence pride themselves on being able to keep going in the face of difficult challenges, frustrations and seemingly insurmountable problems. They understand that success in the face of adversity requires extra effort and determination, so they never give up.", "sec": [["At its best", ["It’s deeply instilled in them to never, ever give up. They achieve many things with this mantra.", "They often find others around them giving up on a task way before they do, if they ever do.", "They’ve been called a dog with a bone sometimes; they just love the feeling of finding a way forward when it seemed there wasn’t one.", "They don’t give up on people. They recognise that we all make mistakes and work hard to retain good relationships with others."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders show perseverance in the face of adversity. They’re always able to find an extra source of motivation when things get really difficult, helping them achieve when others may fail."]], ["In others", ["Help them to see when they may simply need to admit defeat. Help them understand where their priorities lie, and when to direct their focus elsewhere."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not pause in between each tough accomplishment to understand why the task was so difficult. They may miss out on learning opportunities."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What keeps your momentum going when things are challenging?", "How do you know when it is time to re-evaluate and go down a different route?", "What strategies maintain your skill in keeping going?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Keep motivated during tougher or longer-term projects by focusing on the outcomes, purpose and impact. Take regular breaks from high-energy tasks and know when a new course of action may be necessary.", "Work with team members to understand how people are adapting to change and refining their approach. Highlight how the focus should be on the successful end goal for the organisation, not on simply keeping going.", "Regularly discuss any barriers or concerns with stakeholders so that they provide support to find solutions. If a strategy is not successful, it is OK to try a different one and embrace the learning."]], ["In your teams", ["Make sure that the team feel confident enough to say when they need to change direction, or to admit that something isn’t working. Set milestones for reviews to discuss progress."]]]}, {"s": "personal-responsibility", "n": "Personal Responsibility", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Personal Responsibility are always true to their word. If they make a promise or commitment, then they make sure that they keep it. They never blame others but take ownership of everything they do and hold themselves accountable for doing it. They are seen as someone who is always prepared to do what they have promised.", "sec": [["At its best", ["Their colleagues know that they can always count on them. Letting them down isn’t an option.", "They always keep promises to people, no matter what. It’s really important to them that they deliver what they say, when they say they will.", "They work best when they are being held accountable and own the task, however big or small. They enjoy responsibility.", "If they ever make a mistake, it feels awful. They always own up to it and immediately see what they can do to put it right."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders commit to doing what they’ve promised. If leaders don’t know an answer or direction, they’ll do whatever it takes to resolve it. Letting people down is not an option for a leader."]], ["In others", ["Encourage them to share responsibility with their team members when appropriate to avoid them feeling overwhelmed, and to ask for support when it is needed."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may beat themselves up when things don’t go according to plan, which could negatively impact on their wellbeing. They need to allow others to take responsibility sometimes."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What types of activity do you find yourself consistently taking responsibility for?", "What do you love most about taking responsibility for things yourself?", "Are there any situations you struggle to take responsibility for? How do you feel when this happens?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Volunteer to take on sections of larger complex projects at work. Be clear on what will be done as part of this and lead personal priorities for the agreed timescales. Partner with others to find solutions to barriers so obligations are fulfilled.", "Work with others to ensure goals are clear and time bound. Having measurable outcomes and accountability will create direction and enable a quicker result.", "Communicate with stakeholders regularly in a transparent and open way about progress, problems and solutions. Continue to build trust by owning any mistakes and ways in which the project needed to adapt."]], ["In your teams", ["Explore learning new skills as a team by taking on an activity outside of the business area. This could be a more difficult project that others have shelved."]]]}, {"s": "personalisation", "n": "Personalisation", "f": "Relating", "x": "People with a strength in Personalisation notice the subtle differences in people that make them unique. They recognise the different motivators of individual people as well as their likes, dislikes, preferences, skills and talents. They love to relate to everyone as a unique individual, recognising that what is right for one person may not be right for another.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They pick up on the differences in people. They know that to get the best out of a person, they need to treat them as an individual.", "They focus really carefully on people’s needs and then direct their attention accordingly. People always seem surprised with what they pick up from this.", "Everyone has unique capabilities. They enjoy taking their time to get to know what these are and appreciate them. It brings a smile to people’s faces.", "We all want to be known for what we bring. They love that they can tap into this by giving people tasks that match their strengths."]], ["As a leader", ["When building project teams, leaders love to put together a diverse team, knowing this will promote creativity and get the best results for the individual and the business."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of them focusing too much on people’s individuality and losing sight of what they have in common. Help them to understand what brings people together too."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may spend too much time focusing on all the nuances of people or beat themselves up if they miss an important fact about someone."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does your ability to relate to people as unique individuals come from?", "What is it that enables you to personalise an approach with each different person?", "How does the Personalisation skill support your daily work tasks?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Volunteer to get more involved in mentoring colleagues in the organisation. Personalising the approach will see those being mentored become more relaxed and open to learning. Help others to create goals that recognise their greatness.", "Appreciate that each team member will respond differently to success, stress, learning and collaboration. Pay close attention to other people’s strengths and weaknesses to maximise the effectiveness of the whole team.", "Foster stronger relationships by getting to know the team better through informal events or on team days. Spend time understanding the uniqueness of each person, in and out of work, and demonstrate a personalised approach."]], ["In your teams", ["Use these differences to spot training needs and create development opportunities, tailored to individual differences. Would they prefer practical, theory or video tutorials?"]]]}, {"s": "persuasion", "n": "Persuasion", "f": "Relating", "x": "People with a strength in Persuasion are particularly effective at convincing others and bringing them around to their point of view. They love to make a good argument for what they want, choosing their language, words and methods carefully to win agreement from others.", "sec": [["At its best", ["When they require a different approach by someone, they get them to see how good the idea is themselves through their own suggestions or research.", "They find one of the best ways to get someone on board with their ideas is to help them take ownership of it themselves.", "They always know their facts. Reality and a business case is critical in convincing someone to do something they don’t want to do.", "People follow along with them quite easily. They are seen as a good leader, as they are confident enough to suggest to people what they would like."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders enjoy making tough decisions themselves, bringing others around to their leadership view through balanced and considered discussions."]], ["In others", ["Help them to understand when they need to tone down their persuasiveness and take a subtler approach, to gain collaborative agreement from an individual or the team."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not always do what’s needed to ensure that their way is the right way, resulting in them defending or promoting something that lacks research or isn’t proven."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What is most energising about bringing other people round to a different way of thinking?", "What are the ways in which you persuade others successfully?", "In what situations is your Persuasion skill most effective?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Make influencing relevant, and be clear with team members about the intentions behind any persuading. Avoid being seen as simply arguing, but instead as fighting for a good cause for the team, client or organisation.", "Remember to build positive relationships with colleagues, for an occasion in the future when their vote might be needed. Make sure conversations are two way, to get to know colleagues and build trust.", "Being prepared in advance can help with persuasive skills. Access as much information as possible before meetings or writing a response to understand the full argument and facts ahead of putting forward a case to others."]], ["In your teams", ["When delivering messages, winning new business, or gaining support for new ideas, get the team to consider the best approach in their context. Does the audience need more budget or buy-in?"]]]}, {"s": "planner", "n": "Planner", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Planner have a natural ability to plan and prepare, taking a deliberate and systematic approach to everything they do. Before starting things, they think carefully, get organised, establish times, and assess and allocate resources. They love to make sure they have covered all eventualities – including planning for the unexpected.", "sec": [["At its best", ["For them, it’s essential to have a plan before they do anything – they work much better that way. Their working relationships are quite organised too.", "They just love organising events. They put a lot of time and effort into the planning stage because they feel that it is the only way to guarantee success.", "They plan well in advance of any deadline, so they have plenty of time to deal with the unexpected and be satisfied with all the details.", "They make sure that they plan everything to the very last detail, so that nothing is left to chance."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love to think ahead and plan things in advance. They inspire confidence in others through their preparedness for all situations, whether immediate or long-term initiatives."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of when their well-structured plans are restricting their creativity and innovation. Help them to see where a plan may not be necessary and the benefits of flexibility."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not know when to be spontaneous or to appreciate when a systematic approach isn’t the best approach, coming across as resistant to others."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What has made the greatest difference to you by having a concrete plan?", "What is it that is most energising about planning for you?", "How do others benefit from the plans you make?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Be careful not to accept tasks and projects without a deadline or to be the one to put a project plan in place. Share the step-by-step plan to deliver the project so that clients, stakeholders and team members all know the delivery date and there are no surprises.", "Go to the next level with planning by working with team members who love spontaneity and creativity. Work together to build flexibility and fresh ideas into the plans, as well as meeting the deadline. Innovation ideas will be delivered efficiently.", "Set aside a regular time to plan the next working week before taking action. Create a list of what needs to be done and by when, and what or who might need to be organised before then to complete the tasks."]], ["In your teams", ["Assess every task that comes your way and learn to spot when a plan isn’t needed. This allows the team to learn how to enjoy a more spontaneous approach."]]]}, {"s": "prevention", "n": "Prevention", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Prevention love to think ahead and anticipate problems before they happen. They notice the little things that might be out of place or going wrong, and then deal with them. They notice, where others may not, the areas that need attention to prevent future problems.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They believe we can stop a lot of things happening if we consider and plan out tasks and projects properly in advance.", "Whatever task they undertake, they are always on the lookout for challenges and hidden issues. Then they like to make sure that they do something so that issues don’t happen.", "They might look at something and think “That’s an accident waiting to happen”. If they didn’t act on their suspicions, they know it would play on their mind.", "They try to anticipate future problems in their relationships by being as open as possible to stop issues escalating."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders take considerable pride in spotting potential problems in their leadership accountabilities. They see current or future issues that are often missed by their peers or teams."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of them playing it too safe and overlooking the benefits of taking risks. Help them to be more comfortable when situations arise that they have not anticipated."]], ["Hidden risks", ["Thinking ahead all the time could hinder their ability to enjoy the moment or move something to action based on new information available."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What drives your focus on anticipating problems and dealing with them before they happen?", "What resources do you draw on to ensure situations and subsequent interventions are judged correctly?", "How do you use your ability to anticipate and prevent problems in your daily tasks?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Explain the rationale for any caution to stakeholders, to avoid looking too timid or fearful of action. While instincts are usually right, back them up with some calculated risks that stakeholders are also comfortable with.", "Avoid potential fatigue by recognising that it isn’t necessary to take on all the responsibility to prevent issues – instead, act as a sounding board on work initiatives. It’s OK to come up with the ideas for team members, but not always the plan.", "Discuss key challenges with clients and share how problems have been solved in the past. Assure clients that this information will be built on to prevent similar problems from arising in the future."]], ["In your teams", ["Reflect on what has worked well in the past and use this experience to help others anticipate problems before they happen as a team. Share successful templates or plans."]]]}, {"s": "pride", "n": "Pride", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Pride take pride in everything that they do. They love to consistently deliver work that is of the highest standard and quality, getting it right first time, every time. They set high standards for themselves and enjoy other people’s recognition for the quality of what they do.", "sec": [["At its best", ["‘Only the best will do’ is an attitude that they apply to all their work – they believe it’s the only way.", "They are often considered a perfectionist, but it’s just that they consider high quality to be important in everything.", "Whatever they are working on, they like to make it the best it can be, rather than settle for what was asked of them.", "They love to receive praise for a high-quality outcome. If they don’t receive the recognition, they find themselves evaluating what could have made it even better."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders take immense pride in their performance and frequently seek feedback from their teams about how they can run meetings, oversee projects and manage other people’s career development more effectively."]], ["In others", ["Help them to see the value of other people’s involvement to a task, even when this doesn’t perhaps meet their own high standards."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may be a harsh critic, rather than encouraging and teaching people to apply better standards, knowing everyone has a contribution to make."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does your enjoyment of delivering work of the highest quality come from?", "What results does your focus on quality achieve at work?", "How can it be ensured that the quality you strive for is appropriate for its context?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Even if team members’ contributions aren’t to the organisation’s high standards, remember to be more appreciative of the effort, however small the task. Recognise that encouraging quality will see more of it.", "Explore ways to use tools to ensure work is of good quality. Ask colleagues known for taking pride in their work about what processes or tips they use, and commit to work with others to ensure a higher standard across any teamwork.", "Share with team members the expectations of high-quality work, and how they can achieve this. Give them examples and templates of exemplary work to follow. Discuss any common mistakes that have been noticed to avoid repetition."]], ["In your teams", ["Be aware not to spend too long perfecting work as a team. Recognise that there sometimes needs to be a balance between quality and delivery."]]]}, {"s": "rapport-builder", "n": "Rapport Builder", "f": "Relating", "x": "People with a strength in Rapport Builder start conversations with people quickly and easily, including those they are meeting for the first time. They quickly find something that is of interest to them both, then open up the conversation further. They enjoy meeting people for the first time and getting to know them.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They seem to be able to talk to anybody, at any time, about anything.", "They can interact with quiet people easily and get people to smile who otherwise never seem to.", "For them, the starting point of connecting with people is engaging with them in a way that’s comfortable and enjoyable.", "They enjoy meeting strangers and finding common interests and experiences that they can talk about."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders always make time to connect and get to know someone, providing a secure and positive foundation to build upon and for future collaboration."]], ["In others", ["Help them to recognise that people build relationships at different speeds, and the need to take a slower approach at times with some."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not know when to step back and can appear as inauthentic or intrusive. Help them to go beyond small talk when appropriate to build deeper relationships."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What motivates you about getting to know people?", "What makes you effective at building rapport with others?", "Are there any situations where you find it more challenging to build rapport with people? Why is this?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Look out for opportunities to have more diverse interactions, such as across other teams or different stakeholders. When given the chance, aim to put people at ease, charm clients and build future networks.", "While building connections, it is important to get to know stakeholders personally too. Ask questions to find out more about them and their goals, to start to forge deeper relationships.", "Enlist the help of a colleague skilled in building rapport and discuss ways to expand the network of contacts to meet future organisational goals. Identify key individuals or groups to focus on building a rapport with."]], ["In your teams", ["It may benefit the team, on occasion, to go beyond an immediate rapport with clients. Give yourselves extra time to invest in more meaningful connections with important stakeholders."]]]}, {"s": "relationship-deepener", "n": "Relationship Deepener", "f": "Relating", "x": "Building close relationships with others is very important to people with a strength in Relationship Deepener. For them, really getting to know someone and for that person to know them takes time. Their relationships with people will develop slowly but nearly always last over the long term.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They have made a number of strong relationships over the years and love keeping in contact with these people.", "Even if they meet up with a colleague they haven’t seen for some time, it’s like a day hasn’t passed since they saw them.", "If they are meeting with a client or colleague, they try to pop in and see someone else in the area, even if it’s just for a quick coffee.", "Keeping their relationships alive and developing them further is very important to them. They never know when they will need them."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders have a depth of relationships with people that mean they can set challenging personal and professional goals, knowing they have the backing, resources and support from others."]], ["In others", ["Help them to see others may not want to have the same level of depth in relationships as they do. Get them to identify when short-term connections could also be useful."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may spend too much time developing relationships with others to the detriment of important deadlines or their own wellbeing."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What is a great outcome of a relationship you’ve invested in?", "How do you know which relationships to invest in?", "How do you like to keep in touch and build relationships with people?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Consider how to invest more in new client relationships while continuing to build on existing ones. Review the range of relationships and draw up a plan to build a broader client base using social channels, existing contacts and networking events. Keep in regular touch to build strong relationships.", "Volunteer to take on roles where building trust is important, perhaps engaging with disgruntled clients or resolving sensitive problems. Get to know people and the issues they face, going beyond the surface, so that they feel fully understood and valued.", "Reflect on which relationships across the organisation it would be worth building to benefit personal and team goals. Ask peers for help finding the introductions necessary to get started."]], ["In your teams", ["Ask the team to list the more superficial benefits of building relationships. Use this to help them balance when using this skill will be most appropriate."]]]}, {"s": "relationship-manager", "n": "Relationship Manager", "f": "Relating", "x": "People strong in Relationship Manager derive satisfaction from nurturing connections and leverage them as needed to support their work. They are successful in building and maintaining a range of relationships to support their goals and projects. They get to know the drivers and needs of others so they can build effective relationships.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They actively build and maintain a diverse network of contacts, both within and outside their organisation, so that they can call on their expertise when needed.", "They actively participate in networking events, conferences and industry gatherings to expand their network, exchange ideas and stay informed, in order to achieve their goals.", "They maintain regular communication and follow up with their contacts, demonstrating genuine interest and investment in the relationship over time.", "They excel at remembering personal details and preferences, demonstrating thoughtfulness and consideration, so they can build effective relationships."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders with a strength in Relationship Manager get to know what people are working on now and in the future. They like to understand other people’s motivations, both personally and professionally, so they can connect with them on a purposeful level and support their career development."]], ["In others", ["Support others to review the range of their relationships in the organisation, as there may be times when they need to call on the support of colleagues at varying levels for critical issues."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may focus too much time on building relationships in their professional network at the expense of getting on with their project tasks. Encourage them to find a balance between daily tasks and networking with others."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do you successfully navigate working relationships?", "How would you describe the range of your relationships at work?", "How do you get to know people so you can build productive relationships?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Practise active listening to understand the needs, concerns and goals of others. Demonstrate genuine interest in their perspectives to build trust and rapport. Use this empathic approach to connect with others and foster mutually beneficial relationships.", "Appreciate that networking is important. Expand and nurture professional contacts by attending networking events, joining professional associations, and engaging with colleagues, customers and competitors. Maintain regular contact with people and seek opportunities for collaboration and mutual support.", "Prioritise building trust in relationships with clients by demonstrating reliability, integrity and authenticity. Follow through on commitments, in order to deliver on the clients’ goals and strengthen the relationships for the long term."]], ["In your teams", ["Make sure that, as a team, you are balancing both productivity and nurturing relationships. Review interactions with others to establish whether they are two-way as well as productive."]]]}, {"s": "resilience", "n": "Resilience", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Resilience have a terrific ability to overcome adversity. They take hardships and setbacks in their stride. They always find the resources and strength to pick themselves back up, even in the most difficult circumstances. They know that they have the strength to cope with, and recover from, anything that life throws at them.", "sec": [["At its best", ["You can’t keep them down for long – their moto is ‘learn from it and move on’.", "When their suggestions get knocked back, they don’t give up. They find another way to keep on track and get people to listen.", "They find their achievements happened because they kept going when others would have given up.", "They have had their fair share of challenges, but it seems to have made them stronger and better able to cope with things."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders can overcome adversity and seem able to take challenges in their stride without using up lots of energy or losing motivation in the moment."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of them appearing too tough. Encourage them to have time to recover from situations and to understand how they could manage similar things in future."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may become dismiss a situation that others have struggled with, rather than engaging with sympathy and humility where necessary."]], ["Coaching questions", ["When was a significant time you noticed you were resilient, and what was the outcome?", "What enables you to maintain your skill in Resilience through challenging circumstances?", "When has your Resilience skill been most tested to the extreme?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Role model the road to recovery. Talk openly and warmly about challenges and the strategies used for overcoming them. Use stories of overcoming adversity to help inspire team members to keep going.", "When resilience is required, stay informed and adaptable. Don’t jump to conclusions but gather facts, develop a plan and take action, using this as a learning opportunity. Be patient with those less resilient by offering support and a positive attitude.", "When experiencing a setback with a client, take a step back. What needs to be acknowledged and done to satisfy the client? Analyse the situation and understand what can be controlled quickly. Then gather stakeholders to ensure the remedy is future-proof."]], ["In your teams", ["Arrange a team catch-up after any setback, so that they can all understand and interpret it together. What keeps them strong? What can they do even better next time?"]]]}, {"s": "resolver", "n": "Resolver", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Resolver love solving problems – the more complicated the problem, the better. They thrive on getting their teeth into a really complex problem and focusing on getting to the root of it, whatever it might be. They will always go the extra mile to find a solution, being extremely thorough and pursuing all avenues to get a good result.", "sec": [["At its best", ["In their view, every problem has a solution. Nothing gives them greater pleasure than trying to find it.", "They are rarely beaten by a problem, and they love it when they beat a particularly challenging one. They seem to hear the word ‘problem’ a mile off.", "They find they are always getting involved in helping and advising on any problem. It just comes naturally to them to get involved.", "People always come to them for advice on approaches to different situations. They can usually help unlock them, even if it isn’t an area they know much about."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love getting involved in solving problems that people present to them. They enjoy taking the lead on tasks where they can help resolve a pressing issue."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of them spending too much time searching for a solution to a problem. Help them to prioritise their work, ask for help and recognise when it is more appropriate to move on."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not keep an open or creative perspective on approaches, preferring to keep going in a way that has worked before."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What is it about problem-solving that is particularly enjoyable for you?", "When thinking about problems successfully solved, which one are you most proud of?", "What is your best approach to solving challenging problems?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Partner with a team member with the Prevention skill. Try to anticipate future issues by thinking about what possible obstacles may arise in a project. Develop ideas about how future issues could be avoided, downgraded or resolved should they arise.", "Try not to rush with resolving problems for clients. Consider all the options and the people who would need to be involved. Take time to come up with the right strategy to prevent further issues for clients.", "When assessing a challenging issue, seek to acquire as much information as possible, in order to identify the source of a problem and present the best solution. Get team members to support with working through the suggestions to resolve the issue."]], ["In your teams", ["Not all problems are there to be solved. Set a time limit for how long the team should spend on trying to resolve a problem before moving on to a new task or enlisting a fresh perspective."]]]}, {"s": "self-awareness", "n": "Self-awareness", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Self-awareness enjoy spending time and focusing effort on understanding their behaviour, emotions and responses to different situations. They have a deep awareness and understanding of their strengths and weaknesses. By having this self-awareness, their behaviour doesn’t surprise them or catch them off guard.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They live in a very self-reflective way and know their limits, abilities and preferences very well.", "They often question themselves and their motives as they are aware of what they are doing, how they might be perceived, and the way they come across.", "They find they have authentic relationships as they are really happy to change, grow and accept feedback. They do this better than most other people.", "Knowing themselves as they do helps them to be more open to knowing others. They love to find out other people’s strengths and weaknesses too."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders like taking time to reflect on their leadership style and the impact they have on others. They are conscious of how they behave in front of their teams and the impact it may have on them."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of times when they don’t fully understand their reasoning for the actions they take. Help them to reflect on any responses which seem to cause a personal challenge."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may be perceived as self-indulgent, rather than ensuring they balance their focus on both themselves and others, or on the situation or task at hand."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How has your knowledge of emotions and behaviour developed over time?", "How is your skill in Self-awareness used to support daily tasks?", "What strategies can you use to build and maintain a high level of self-awareness?"]], ["Upskilling", ["To further understand how behaviour affects others, ask for feedback from someone new, to get a diverse opinion. They may see things differently and provide an alternative view. Take note of their views to maintain a high level of self-awareness.", "Encourage a feedback and learning environment at work. Colleagues may avoid both positive and negative feedback, so develop a culture where everyone feels safe to spot positive and negative actions, to encourage greatness in teams.", "Proactively review or discuss the week with colleagues. Which tasks were challenging and which were effortless? Why was this? Which strengths and weaknesses were being used? Keep a journal of the answers to remain self-aware."]], ["In your teams", ["Use their strength in Self-awareness to take on roles within the team that will push them to develop their skills further. Have team catch-ups regularly that ensure people are working on the tasks that motivate them."]]]}, {"s": "self-belief", "n": "Self-belief", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Self-belief are very confident and self-assured, with a firm belief in their own skills and abilities. With this self-confidence, they always believe that they can achieve the goals they are working towards. They believe that almost anything is within their reach if they set their mind to it and work hard to achieve it.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They have a ‘can-do’ attitude to life. They believe if you don’t try, then you will never know what you could have achieved.", "They find that their self-belief instils a confidence in the people around them, which is very satisfying.", "They know that they are good at influencing, so they are often asked to speak up or manage situations that others don’t want to.", "They never think they are going to fail when they go into a situation. They are comfortable to go and get on with it, knowing they will find a way."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders take difficulty in their stride, confident that if they don’t know how to do something or have the immediate answer, it won’t be long before they do know."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of times when their sense of self-belief can come across as overconfident. Encourage them to balance this with humility, by considering how others might view them."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not always enlist the help of others, feeling like they know the answer. It might feel like they don’t always value the collaborative efforts of people."]], ["Coaching questions", ["Where does the confidence in your ability to do things come from?", "What enables you to maintain and sustain your belief in your abilities?", "In what situation has your skill of Self-belief been most challenged?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Get out there leading, selling to or influencing clients or stakeholders, remembering to adapt the approach to different audiences. Note and share what went well for repeat success, along with opportunities for team learnings.", "Strive to be a role model for team members around confidence. Offer to take ownership of difficult conversations or situations, and guide colleagues to solutions and to share perspectives that encourage self-belief.", "Set aside more time to review progress and keep a record of achievements, however small. Take opportunities to share the efforts and successes with colleagues or managers. Ask them for support to remain on track with the chosen goals."]], ["In your teams", ["At the end of projects, ask the team to nominate an individual who they think was particularly successful. Build regular self-belief across all team members."]]]}, {"s": "service", "n": "Service", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a skill in Service feel compelled to help others as much as they can. They get a great deal of satisfaction when they have helped someone. They strive to go above and beyond what they need to do, often exceeding people’s expectations. They are focused entirely on satisfying people’s needs and meeting their requirements.", "sec": [["At its best", ["Nothing is too much trouble. They really love to help people and they just can’t stop themselves from doing it.", "People are often surprised by the level of help they provide. If you need help, they will do whatever it takes, at whatever time, and little gets in their way.", "They take time to listen to people’s needs, get any information they need, sort out immediate problems, and then often come back to people to check if there is anything else.", "People appreciate them for what they do for others, but if they didn’t, they would do it anyway – they just can’t help helping!"]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders thrive in stressful situations by focusing on what a successful end result will mean to clients. This becomes their driver for successful project delivery."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of their focus on other people’s needs at the expense of their own. Encourage them to take time to rest, and to serve their own needs as well."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not always help people learning to sort their own needs out, too often being on hand and taking care of things."]], ["Coaching questions", ["In what situations are you motivated the most to help others?", "What makes you most happy when serving others?", "How do you decide who to reach out to and go the extra mile for?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Work with a colleague who likes to help others, to consider the customer journey of a product or service. What is the customer’s feedback or experience of the organisation? Together, come up with a list of ideas to improve the service provided.", "Encourage a culture in the team where it is OK to ask for help, and people know where to go and who to ask to get it. Explore ways for team members to successfully collaborate and complement and support each other by sharing strengths and weaknesses.", "Be a role model who goes above and beyond to support colleagues. Offer to coach colleagues and support them on their journey of professional development. Look for opportunities to put them in touch with others who can help too."]], ["In your teams", ["When helping others, think about whether the help can be applied to the team as a whole. Try to share the advice with everyone, either by demonstration or through an email."]]]}, {"s": "social-adaptability", "n": "Social Adaptability", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People strong in Social Adaptability have an innate way to tailor their communication approaches and way of behaving to suit their audiences. They enjoy connecting with people on their level and adjust their tone and language accordingly. They keenly observe their environment and read the signs to change their interactions.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They keenly observe their environment and the people around them, picking up on subtle cues and signals about what is important to others.", "They are skilled listeners, paying close attention to the verbal and non-verbal cues of clients, so that they can connect and understand each other.", "They are culturally sensitive and aware, adapting their approach to recognise and respect the cultural expectations of different social groups.", "They excel at building rapport with others, by being socially aware and adapting their communication and behaviour depending on the audience."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders with a strength of Social Adaptability know how to read the room for subtle cues. They adapt their interactions to be able to meet the needs of their team by responding in a way they understand and that motivates them."]], ["In others", ["Support others to sometimes just enjoy the moment and be natural, rather than thinking what the perfect style should be when interacting with others."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may overthink the way they wish to be perceived, in the hope of appealing to their entire audience. Get them to consider that being themselves can be a great way to connect with others."]], ["Coaching questions", ["How do you decide to change your approach with people?", "What approaches to adapting communication styles have you taken?", "Are there any situations or people for which you find it more challenging to change communication styles?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Try different approaches, as this could increase the impact of communication with team members. Perhaps try being more passive or assertive, or mirroring them, for example.", "Observe the behaviour of others, paying attention to social cues, body language and non-verbal communication. This can help better understand the dynamics of a situation and how behaviour may need to be adapted. Note any variances with different types of stakeholders.", "Be open to adapting communication style, tone and approach based on the personalities and preferences of the people being interacted with. Being culturally sensitive will help avoid misunderstandings and show respect."]], ["In your teams", ["Adapting to each other in the team will establish a strong and positive culture. Be mindful to uphold a universally accepted culture too so you can relax together and enjoy common ground."]]]}, {"s": "spotlight", "n": "Spotlight", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Spotlight enjoy being the centre of attention. Whether in a meeting or social gathering, they naturally speak up and hold the floor. They like holding people’s interest and focus. They can get people to listen to them and keep their attention – whatever else might be going on.", "sec": [["At its best", ["Performing and talking is what they are all about. They just love being the centre of attention and having people focusing on them.", "They get a real buzz from being listened to. They try to do this in an engaging way, so others enjoy what they have to say.", "Any opportunity they get, they entertain people and make them laugh through stories, experiences and jokes.", "They are often more comfortable with public speaking, or speaking in large groups, than they are with a one-to-one conversation."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love being in situations where the focus is on them and what they’re saying. This gives them the opportunity to lead the agenda and influence the direction of others."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of times they may need to step back and give others attention, allowing others to develop this skill and share in being the focus of attention."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may not allow others their time in the spotlight as well to share their ideas or concerns, sometimes speaking up at the expense of others."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What underpins your desire to be in the spotlight?", "What is enjoyable about speaking up in front of others for you?", "How is your Spotlight skill used to achieve objectives?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Pay attention to the needs of diverse audiences and deliver a tailored presentation to engage clients or team members. Build resources on a range of tools like compelling stories and videos, rather than read off notes or a PowerPoint.", "Remember, when in front of a client, it’s as important to know how to say something as it is what to say. Seek feedback from colleagues to ensure contributions are relevant and timely, and content is well informed to excite clients.", "Offer to speak out about different achievements and experiences to help team members learn and to acknowledge individual successes. Add value to those who often don’t get recognised."]], ["In your teams", ["Present their work as a team to other departments or senior leaders. Perhaps focus on a challenging topic as a team and involve every member."]]]}, {"s": "strategic-awareness", "n": "Strategic Awareness", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People with a strength in Strategic Awareness have a keen interest in understanding changes in the wider world that could impact their plans and objectives. This big-picture awareness enables them to develop and shift long-term plans effectively. They love to take steps to deal with whatever circumstances might arise in the future.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are always asking themselves and others “What does this mean for us?”.", "They love to think about what the future may bring. They try to picture what might be coming and what this means for their plans.", "They watch the news, read informed comments, study industry journals, track trends and pay attention to anomalies to inform their thinking.", "They have a view on what’s going to happen next on topics and what it means for them and their work."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders are always curious about how trends or changes might affect the current and future projects they’re working on with the team and across their peer group."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of them being too focused on long-term goals. Help them understand that a balance between the short-, medium- and long-term goals is essential for the business to succeed."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may only focus on the bigger picture, which means they can sometimes overlook the need to focus on the smaller details or immediate decision making."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What do you enjoy about focusing on the bigger picture?", "How do you apply your strategic mindset in your role?", "What has been a great achievement by applying your Strategic Awareness skill?"]], ["Upskilling", ["When sharing a future vision, appreciate that it may come across as questioning or objecting to team members’ ideas, people who are focused in the here and now. Take the time to explain the rationale and evidence for questions and objections.", "Boost motivation by partnering up with others with skills in Action, Planner and Resolver to execute strategic ideas and deliver on some of the more immediate milestones at the same time.", "Discuss with a colleague or a manager professional goals and tasks beyond the immediate future. When setting objectives, list the benefits to the business in the short, medium and long term, and how they are contributing to these."]], ["In your teams", ["Get the team to communicate to each other about what they understand the bigger picture to be. How does it fit with their tasks? Ensure that everyone in the team has a clear vision."]]]}, {"s": "technology-focus", "n": "Technology Focus", "f": "Thinking", "x": "People strong in Technology Focus thrive on immersing themselves in the ever-evolving picture of cutting-edge technologies. With their tech passion, they uncover the latest innovations within the tech landscape and are eager to embrace and advocate the changing advancements that could make a difference.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are passionate advocates for the adoption of innovative technologies and innovations within their organisation or industry. They educate others about the potential benefits and applications.", "They actively participate in technology communities, forums and events to connect with like-minded individuals and share knowledge and insights, so that they stay on top of trends.", "They are naturally curious about technology and its potential applications to resolve work challenges, so that client satisfaction can be maintained.", "They mentor and coach others on new technologies and tools, sharing their knowledge and providing guidance, in order to upskill the technology expertise in the organisation."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders with a strength in Technology Focus love continuous learning when it comes to emerging technology. They seek out opportunities through publications, conferences and blogs to understand the latest trends and happily share them with those they lead."]], ["In others", ["Support others to do their due diligence when assessing new software solutions, so that they make informed decisions and minimise risks when implementing new technology."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They may overly focus on looking for new technologies to use to innovate their solutions. Get them to consider whether existing technologies may be more suitable than trying to constantly implement newer ones."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What type of technology is the most enjoyable to research for you?", "At work, what technology would be the most helpful to learn more about and why?", "What has been the greatest benefit to you when technology has been applied within a project?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Read, subscribe, listen to or watch news articles about developments in technology and seek opportunities to become familiar with, and experiment with, themes. This will help to keep knowledge up to date.", "Look for potential opportunities to introduce new technology into the working day, the team or wider organisation. Get feedback from others. Become familiar with the pros and cons of the new application and how it might enhance work and improve daily tasks.", "Make use of new technologies or innovations as much as possible. Practise using different software, hardware or digital tools regularly to build up skills. Set aside dedicated time for practice."]], ["In your teams", ["Some people will be more interested in technology than others and quicker to pick up new applications and software when introduced. In a team, these people can support the others in moving quickly to adopt new technology."]]]}, {"s": "time-optimiser", "n": "Time Optimiser", "f": "Thinking", "x": "Time is very precious to people with a strength in Time Optimiser and they never waste it. They love to squeeze as much as they can into every minute of every day, organising themselves so that they use their time wisely and productively. Whatever situation they are in, they make the most of their time.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They are always thinking “What can I be doing at this moment to make the best use of my time?”.", "Others look at them and wonder how they seem to achieve so much, even picking up extra tasks to their already busy schedule.", "They would much prefer to do something rather than to think or talk about something. They believe we achieve so much more this way.", "Wasting time is not an option. Multi-tasking just seems to come naturally to them."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love planning their time so they can fit in as many things as possible. They pride themselves on being productive and making the most of every leadership opportunity."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of the signs of burnout and the causes of this. Encourage them to relax during quieter periods. Remind them that when they are well rested, they will be more productive in the longer term."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They can be unaware that they are sometimes a bit exhausting for others to watch. They don’t necessarily slow down and allow others to catch up."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What motivates you on optimising your use of time?", "How do you make the most of your time each day?", "In what situations or contexts could you afford to relax your focus on time?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Put in a calendar reminder to check in regularly with progress on tasks. This will avoid rushing things and ensure progress is on track and instructions have been understood, as well as provide an opportunity to check in for inevitable changes.", "Find ways to partner with those who enjoy strategic thinking or planning to complement skills in Time Optimiser. Have frequent informal meetings, so that as well as efficiency being maximised, all aspects of the task are considered.", "Arrange to meet regularly with managers to check that the right things are being worked on at the right time. This will also ensure all the necessary resources are available to get going on tasks."]], ["In your teams", ["Create a wish list of tasks that the team would love to carry out, such as reviewing projects, developing templates and exploring better ways of working. Then, any time a gap appears, they are ready to go!"]]]}, {"s": "unconditionality", "n": "Unconditionality", "f": "Being", "x": "People with a strength in Unconditionality naturally accept people for who and what they are, without ever judging them. They watch and accept people with respect, as they believe that everyone is valuable in their own right. No matter what people may have done, they accept everyone the same – unconditionally.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They’ll often defend someone’s actions when other people are judging them. They believe something must have led them to behave like that.", "They believe we all make mistakes and it’s important to accept people, whatever they have done. No one is perfect.", "In their view, it’s important to accept someone’s faults as well as their good qualities. Our experiences make us who we are.", "Their colleagues likely talk to them about their problems, as they don’t judge their situations or what they have done. They are trusted."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love to encourage those they lead to provide honest feedback, to allow them to be a better leader. Leaders foster trust so that their teams realise they won’t be judged for their opinions."]], ["In others", ["Reassure others that they won’t be perceived as being judgemental if they have an opinion about someone and need to share their views on important matters."]], ["Hidden risks", ["As their door is always open (and always used), they can sometimes fail to ensure their relationships are two way."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What lies at the root of your skill in seeing the good in everyone?", "In what ways do you show your appreciation and acceptance of others?", "How does your acceptance of people benefit you at work?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Explore opportunities to take ownership or influence more of the decision-making in the team. Step in to make sure all views are considered and not judged in the process, especially those of colleagues who avoid speaking up for themselves.", "Make a conscious effort to see the good in everyone and acknowledge that everyone values things differently. Behaviour often comes about through trying to meet people’s needs, so dive deeper and understand their journeys.", "Commit to speaking up more in work settings when there is a feeling a team member is being judged. Offer to share personal knowledge and views – in time, it may shift the mindset of work colleagues."]], ["In your teams", ["While it is great to accept people for who they are, ensure the team discuss how they think their behaviour links to their goals overall, so that people are all aligned in their focus."]]]}, {"s": "work-ethic", "n": "Work Ethic", "f": "Motivating", "x": "People with a strength in Work Ethic are extremely hard workers, putting a lot of effort and energy into their work. They enjoy putting in extra hours and they are capable of working longer – and over a longer period of time – than most other people. They are aware that they work much harder than others and they enjoy doing it.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They believe you get out what you put in. They naturally throw themselves into their work.", "They always work hard – it’s likely been instilled in them that hard work pays off.", "Whatever they are doing, they love to be busy. Even on a day off or at the weekend, they will have hobbies that keep them working hard.", "They look forward to tasks where they know they are going to have to put in a lot of effort and hours. The result is so much more rewarding and satisfying."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love putting in extra effort to be an effective leader, even in situations where they know it won’t be easy. Clients and customers can rely on leaders to go the extra mile for them, whatever time of day."]], ["In others", ["Be mindful of them focusing solely on work. Help them recognise when they have worked hard enough and need to slow down, to avoid a negative impact on their wellbeing."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They sometimes need others to remind them to spend more time socialising with colleagues. They can easily let work be the only focus."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What drives the strong work ethic in you?", "When do you experience the most satisfaction from putting in long hours?", "What impact does your strong work ethic have on you and those around you?"]], ["Upskilling", ["Set aside time to regularly evaluate whether the right things are being worked on at the right time. With the amount of effort that may be required, working on the wrong tasks is to be avoided.", "Re-energise the skill of Work Ethic by taking time out to connect with others and enjoy the success of any periods of hard work. Collaborating with colleagues is important to continue to get projects completed efficiently with others.", "Discuss and identify any obstacles that may prevent going that extra mile on a project. Ensure goals are clear and that there are sufficient resources to achieve these goals."]], ["In your teams", ["Be careful the team don’t get burned out. Have a team discussion on why the long hours are necessary and whether this is sustainable. Take a break when necessary."]]]}, {"s": "writer", "n": "Writer", "f": "Communicating", "x": "People with a strength in Writer love to write, finding a deep fulfilment in playing with words and the joys of written language. They have a natural ability to communicate through writing. The act of writing helps them to clarify their thoughts and they write clearly and easily. They take positive pleasure in words and love to write things for others to read.", "sec": [["At its best", ["They love to write about anything, at any time. They find it relaxing and love to articulate their thoughts and ideas.", "They spend time carefully crafting their written communications. Even their text messages use the right words.", "Time seems to go so fast when they are writing. They can get lost in the message that they wanted to put across and the words just flow.", "They use the stories, challenges and experiences of people they meet as part of their writing. It helps them bring things to life."]], ["As a leader", ["Leaders love to express themselves through writing. They write down their ideas to help them clarify their thoughts and get their point across in the best way possible to those they lead."]], ["In others", ["Encourage them to recognise the value of engaging in verbal communication as well as written, when it’s appropriate to encourage more social interaction."]], ["Hidden risks", ["They can fail to realise that not everyone likes to read something – some people prefer a call or voice note."]], ["Coaching questions", ["What sort of writing do you enjoy most?", "What was the last piece of written work that you were proud of? What was the outcome?", "How does your writing ability help you achieve your daily tasks?"]], ["Upskilling", ["When writing to a stakeholder, take into consideration what will be most useful for them. Do they prefer lots of information or high-level points? Do they prefer a formal or informal tone of language? Adjust the writing style for the given stakeholder.", "Before any writing, take a moment to consider the audiences. While the consideration may be to write a thoughtful email or blog, the audience may prefer a quote, an image or some simple bullet-point facts.", "Ask people to provide feedback to keep viewers engaged. What do clients or team members enjoy, what gets the most ‘likes’ or shares, and how is the use of words received? Find ways to keep the dictionary interesting."]], ["In your teams", ["As they write communications, encourage them to choose their words wisely for the audience and the context. Make sure that the team don’t sacrifice quality and clarity for lengthy communications."]]]}];
const LIBRARY = LIBRARY_FULL.map(o=>[o.n,o.f,o.x]);
const FAMILIES = {Being:"#9275FA", Thinking:"#487BFA", Motivating:"#FDB601", Communicating:"#FB6CF5", Relating:"#262626"};
const famCol = f => FAMILIES[f] || "#8a8a8a";
function skillLine(s){ return s[2]; }
